// Rep Max questions: Design and Technology (AQA 8552), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"gdt-001","s":"dt","t":"3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"What is planned obsolescence?","o":["Designing a product so it can be upgraded for many years","Designing a product so its useful life is deliberately limited","Making a product only from recycled materials","Making products in a factory with no workers"],"a":1,"x":"Planned obsolescence makes people replace products sooner, for example by short battery life or by regular new models. It creates waste and uses more resources.","w":["That is the opposite: planned obsolescence shortens a product's life, not extends it.","","Mixes it up with sustainable design; recycled materials say nothing about product life.","Confuses it with automation; it is about limited product life, not who makes it."]}
{"id":"gdt-002","s":"dt","t":"3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Why is a CNC router (CAM) useful for making a batch of 200 identical chair parts?","o":["It removes the need for any digital design","It makes each part slightly different for variety","It works without any setting up or checking","It cuts every part to the same accurate size from a digital file"],"a":3,"x":"Computer-aided manufacture follows a CAD file, so every part is cut to the same accurate shape. It is fast, repeatable and accurate for batch production.","w":["CNC still needs a CAD file to follow, so digital design is essential.","CNC repeats the same file, so parts are identical, not varied.","CNC machines need setting up and checking, such as tool and material position.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-003","s":"dt","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"How does a pumped storage hydroelectric scheme store energy?","o":["Water is pumped uphill at low demand and sent through turbines at high demand","Stored water is burned to make steam at times of peak demand","Water is pumped uphill at high demand and sent through turbines at low demand","Electricity is stored in the water as chemical energy"],"a":0,"x":"Spare electricity when demand is low pumps water up to a high reservoir, storing gravitational potential energy. At peak demand the water flows back down through turbines to generate electricity.","w":["","Water is not burned; steam comes from thermal power stations, not hydro.","Timing reversed: pumping uses spare power at low demand, not high demand.","It stores gravitational potential energy, not chemical energy."]}
{"id":"gdt-004","s":"dt","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a smart material?","o":["MDF","Glass reinforced plastic (GRP)","Thermochromic pigment","Kevlar"],"a":2,"x":"Smart materials react to a change in their environment, such as heat, light or pressure. MDF is a manufactured board, GRP is a composite and Kevlar is a technical textile.","w":["MDF is just a manufactured board and does not react to its environment.","GRP is a composite, strong but not reactive to heat or light.","","Kevlar is a strong technical textile, but it does not change with its surroundings."]}
{"id":"gdt-005","s":"dt","t":"3.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Why is carbon fibre reinforced polymer (CFRP) described as a composite material?","o":["It is a single pure element found in nature","It combines carbon fibres with a resin to give high strength for low weight","It is a metal alloy made of carbon and iron","It is a smart material that changes shape when heated"],"a":1,"x":"A composite is made from two or more different materials joined to give properties neither has alone. CFRP is strong and light, so it is used for bike frames and aircraft parts.","w":["Composites are made from two or more materials, not a single pure element.","","CFRP is carbon fibre in resin, not a metal alloy of carbon and iron.","CFRP does not change shape with heat; it is a composite, not a smart material."]}
{"id":"gdt-006","s":"dt","t":"3.1.4","lv":1,"q":"A security light has a PIR sensor, a microcontroller and a lamp. Which part is the output?","o":["The PIR sensor","The microcontroller","The mains power supply","The lamp"],"a":3,"x":"In a systems approach the sensor is the input, the microcontroller is the process and the lamp is the output.","w":["The PIR sensor detects movement, so it is the input, not the output.","The microcontroller is the process stage, deciding what happens.","The power supply provides energy; it is not the output that does the job.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-007","s":"dt","t":"3.1.5","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is an example of a first order lever, with the pivot between the effort and the load?","o":["A pair of scissors","A wheelbarrow","A fishing rod","A pair of tweezers"],"a":0,"x":"In a first order lever the pivot is between effort and load, as in scissors or a seesaw. A wheelbarrow is second order (load in the middle). Fishing rods and tweezers are third order (effort in the middle).","w":["","A wheelbarrow is second order: the load sits between the pivot and the effort.","A fishing rod is third order: the effort is between the pivot and the load.","Tweezers are third order: the effort is applied between the pivot and the load."]}
{"id":"gdt-008","s":"dt","t":"3.1.5","lv":3,"q":"A 15-tooth driver gear turns clockwise at 120 rpm. It drives a 30-tooth idler gear, which drives a 45-tooth output gear. How does the output gear turn?","o":["40 rpm anticlockwise","60 rpm anticlockwise","40 rpm clockwise","360 rpm clockwise"],"a":2,"x":"An idler does not change the speed ratio, so output speed = 120 × 15 ÷ 45 = 40 rpm. It does reverse direction: driver clockwise, idler anticlockwise, output clockwise.","w":["Speed is right, but each mesh reverses direction: idler anticlockwise, output clockwise.","60 rpm uses the idler's 30 teeth, but an idler is ignored; direction is also wrong.","","Ratio inverted: 120 × 45 ÷ 15 speeds the output up, but a bigger gear turns slower."]}
{"id":"gdt-009","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.1","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a non-ferrous metal?","o":["Low carbon steel","Aluminium","Cast iron","High carbon steel"],"a":1,"x":"Ferrous metals contain iron. Low carbon steel, cast iron and high carbon steel are all iron-based. Aluminium does not contain iron, so it is non-ferrous.","w":["Low carbon steel is an iron alloy, so it is ferrous.","","Cast iron contains iron, so it is ferrous.","High carbon steel is iron-based, so it is ferrous."]}
{"id":"gdt-010","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.2","lv":1,"q":"Which property describes a material that can be hammered or pressed into shape without cracking?","o":["Ductility","Toughness","Hardness","Malleability"],"a":3,"x":"Malleable materials can be hammered or rolled into shape. Ductile materials can be drawn into wire. Toughness is resistance to impact, and hardness is resistance to scratching or denting.","w":["Ductility is being drawn into wire, not hammered or pressed into shape.","Toughness is resisting impact without breaking, not being shaped.","Hardness is resisting scratching or denting, not being formed.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-011","s":"dt","t":"3.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A designer chooses timber that carries the FSC logo. Which factor mainly influenced this choice?","o":["Environmental and ethical sourcing","Lowest possible cost","Highest possible strength","Quickest delivery time"],"a":0,"x":"FSC certified timber comes from forests managed responsibly, which is an environmental and ethical consideration. Certification does not guarantee the lowest cost or highest strength.","w":["","FSC certification is about responsible forestry, not about the cheapest timber.","FSC says nothing about strength; it covers how the forest is managed.","FSC does not affect delivery; it is a sourcing label."]}
{"id":"gdt-012","s":"dt","t":"3.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A diving board is fixed at one end and a diver stands at the free end. Which statement describes the forces in the board?","o":["The top surface is in compression and the bottom surface is in tension","Both surfaces are in compression","The top surface is in tension and the bottom surface is in compression","Both surfaces are in torsion"],"a":2,"x":"A fixed one-ended beam bends downwards under the load. The top surface is stretched (tension) and the bottom is squashed (compression).","w":["Reversed: the board bends down, so the top stretches (tension), not compresses.","The surfaces cannot both be in compression; one side stretches while the other squashes.","","Torsion is twisting; a downward load on the free end causes bending."]}
{"id":"gdt-013","s":"dt","t":"3.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of the six Rs means choosing not to buy a product or material that harms the environment?","o":["Reduce","Refuse","Repair","Recycle"],"a":1,"x":"The six Rs are Rethink, Refuse, Reduce, Reuse, Repair and Recycle. Refuse means saying no to products or materials that cause unnecessary harm.","w":["Reduce means using less, not saying no to a harmful product.","","Repair means fixing something to keep it going, not declining to buy it.","Recycle means reprocessing used material, not refusing to buy it."]}
{"id":"gdt-014","s":"dt","t":"3.2.4","lv":2,"q":"What does a life cycle assessment (LCA) of a product look at?","o":["Only the cost of making it","Only how it is made in the factory","How popular it is with customers","Its environmental impact from raw materials to disposal"],"a":3,"x":"An LCA looks at every stage: getting raw materials, manufacture, transport, use and disposal or recycling, to measure the total environmental impact.","w":["Cost is only one factor; an LCA measures environmental impact, not price.","Manufacture is just one stage; an LCA covers raw materials to disposal.","Popularity is market research, not environmental impact.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-015","s":"dt","t":"3.2.5","lv":1,"q":"Why is timber seasoned before it is used to make furniture?","o":["To reduce its moisture content so it is less likely to warp or split","To make it darker in colour","To increase its moisture content so it bends easily","To make it grow faster"],"a":0,"x":"Freshly cut timber contains a lot of water. Seasoning dries it, which stops it shrinking, warping and splitting after the product is made.","w":["","Seasoning dries timber; it is not done to change its colour.","Reversed: seasoning lowers moisture, and wet timber warps and splits.","Seasoning is done to timber already cut; it does not make anything grow."]}
{"id":"gdt-016","s":"dt","t":"3.2.6","lv":3,"q":"A 2400 mm × 1200 mm sheet is cut into 500 mm × 400 mm panels, all the same way round (none turned). Ignore the width of the saw cuts. What is the greatest number of whole panels?","o":["14","15","12","8"],"a":2,"x":"4 panels fit along 2400 (4 × 500) and 3 along 1200 (3 × 400), so 12. The other way round gives 6 × 2 = 12 too. Dividing areas gives 14.4, but whole panels must fit along each edge.","w":["Divided areas (14.4), but whole panels must fit along each edge.","Rounded 4.8 up to 5; only 4 whole 500 mm panels fit along 2400 mm.","","1200 mm fits three 400 mm panels, not two, so 4 × 3 = 12."]}
{"id":"gdt-017","s":"dt","t":"3.2.7","lv":2,"q":"A factory makes 500 chairs of one style, then changes the machine settings to make 500 of a different style. What type of production is this?","o":["One-off production","Batch production","Mass production","Continuous production"],"a":1,"x":"Batch production makes a limited number of identical products, then changes to another product. One-off makes a single product. Mass and continuous production run the same product without changeover.","w":["One-off production makes a single product, not 500 chairs.","","Mass production runs one product non-stop, with no changeover.","Continuous production runs non-stop on one product; here the settings change."]}
{"id":"gdt-018","s":"dt","t":"3.2.8","lv":2,"q":"What is the main purpose of a jig?","o":["To draw the outline of a shape on the material","To measure the surface finish of a part","To heat a material before it is bent","To hold the work and guide the tool so every part is made the same"],"a":3,"x":"A jig holds the work and guides the cutting tool, which gives repeatable accuracy. A template is used to mark out a shape.","w":["Marking an outline is a template's job; a jig holds work and guides the tool.","Measuring surface finish has nothing to do with a jig.","Heating before bending is not a jig's job; a jig holds and guides.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-019","s":"dt","t":"3.2.9","lv":1,"q":"Which treatment protects timber used outdoors by forcing a preservative deep into it under pressure?","o":["Tanalising","Seasoning","Galvanising","Annealing"],"a":0,"x":"Tanalising forces preservative into timber under pressure, protecting it from rot and insects. Seasoning dries timber, galvanising coats steel with zinc and annealing softens metal by heating it.","w":["","Seasoning dries timber; it does not force preservative in.","Galvanising coats steel with zinc, not timber.","Annealing softens metal by heating; it is not a timber treatment."]}
{"id":"gdt-020","s":"dt","t":"3.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is primary data about what users want from a product?","o":["A magazine article about similar products","Online reviews of a rival product","Results of a questionnaire you give to potential users","A published report of a national survey"],"a":2,"x":"Primary data is collected first-hand by the designer, for example by interviews and questionnaires. Articles, reviews and published surveys are secondary data.","w":["A magazine article is someone else's work, so it is secondary data.","Reviews are written by others, so they are secondary data.","","A published report was collected by others, so it is secondary data."]}
{"id":"gdt-021","s":"dt","t":"3.3.2","lv":1,"q":"What does the Fairtrade mark on a product show?","o":["The product is made from recycled materials","Producers were paid a fair price and had better working conditions","The product was made in the UK","The product has no carbon footprint"],"a":1,"x":"Fairtrade is about social and economic fairness: fair prices and better working conditions for producers, often in developing countries.","w":["That describes a recycled-content label, not Fairtrade.","","Fairtrade is about producers' pay and conditions, not where it was made.","Fairtrade makes no zero-carbon claim; it is about fair pay and conditions."]}
{"id":"gdt-022","s":"dt","t":"3.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Which company is best known for the bagless vacuum cleaner that uses cyclonic separation?","o":["Alessi","Apple","Anglepoise","Dyson"],"a":3,"x":"James Dyson developed the bagless cyclonic vacuum cleaner. Alessi is known for kitchenware, Apple for consumer electronics and Anglepoise for its adjustable lamp.","w":["Alessi is known for kitchenware and design objects, not vacuum cleaners.","Apple is known for consumer electronics, not cyclonic vacuums.","Anglepoise is known for its adjustable lamp, not vacuum cleaners.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-023","s":"dt","t":"3.3.4","lv":2,"q":"A designer keeps returning to her first idea and cannot think of different solutions. What is this called?","o":["Design fixation","Iterative design","User-centred design","Collaboration"],"a":0,"x":"Design fixation is getting stuck on one idea. Iterative design means repeatedly testing and improving. User-centred design focuses on the needs of users.","w":["","Iterative design means testing and improving repeatedly, the opposite of being stuck.","User-centred design focuses on user needs; it is not about being stuck on one idea.","Collaboration is working with others, not being stuck on one idea."]}
{"id":"gdt-024","s":"dt","t":"3.3.5","lv":2,"q":"In a third angle orthographic drawing, where is the plan (top) view drawn in relation to the front view?","o":["Below the front view","To the left of the front view","Above the front view","To the right of the front view"],"a":2,"x":"In third angle projection each view is placed on the same side as the face it shows, so the plan view is above the front view. The right side view is drawn to the right.","w":["Below is the first angle position; in third angle the plan goes above.","Left is for the left side view, not the plan.","","Right is for the right side view, not the plan."]}
{"id":"gdt-025","s":"dt","t":"3.3.6","lv":1,"q":"What is the main reason for making a prototype?","o":["To sell to customers as the final product","To test the design and find problems before production","To avoid needing a design specification","To increase the number of products made"],"a":1,"x":"A prototype lets designers test function, fit and appearance and make improvements before spending money on production.","w":["Confuses a prototype with the finished product; it is a test model, not for sale.","","A prototype supports the specification, it does not replace it.","Prototypes test one design; they do not increase how many products are made."]}
{"id":"gdt-026","s":"dt","t":"3.3.7","lv":2,"q":"Why might a designer choose standard components, such as ready-made hinges and screws, instead of designing new ones?","o":["They can be made to any size needed","They avoid any need for fixings","They are always made from the lightest material","They are cheaper and easy to replace"],"a":3,"x":"Standard components are made in large numbers, so they cost less and are easy to buy and replace. This reduces cost and saves design time.","w":["Mixes this up with custom parts; standard components come in fixed sizes.","Hinges and screws are themselves fixings, so fixings are still needed.","Standard parts are made from many materials; they are not always the lightest.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-027","s":"dt","t":"3.3.8","lv":3,"q":"A part is drawn as 60 mm with a tolerance of +0.3 mm and −0.1 mm. Which range of lengths is acceptable?","o":["59.9 mm to 60.3 mm","59.7 mm to 60.1 mm","59.7 mm to 60.3 mm","59.9 mm to 60.1 mm"],"a":0,"x":"Upper limit = 60 + 0.3 = 60.3 mm. Lower limit = 60 − 0.1 = 59.9 mm. A part within these limits is acceptable.","w":["","Swapped the tolerances: took 0.3 off and added 0.1 instead of the reverse.","Used the larger 0.3 mm tolerance in both directions; the lower one is 0.1 mm.","Used the smaller 0.1 mm tolerance both ways; the upper tolerance is 0.3 mm."]}
{"id":"gdt-028","s":"dt","t":"3.3.9","lv":1,"q":"Why does a designer nest shapes close together when marking out a sheet of material?","o":["To make the shapes stronger","To increase the number of cuts needed","To reduce waste","To leave the sheet looking neater"],"a":2,"x":"Nesting fits shapes together so less material is left over, which saves cost and resources.","w":["Nesting only changes the layout; it does nothing to the strength of the shapes.","Nesting often shares cut lines, so it does not add cuts; it saves material.","","Neatness is not the reason; the aim is to save material and cost."]}
{"id":"gdt-029","s":"dt","t":"3.3.10","lv":1,"q":"Which hand tool is best for cutting tight curves in thin timber?","o":["Tenon saw","Coping saw","Hacksaw","Panel saw"],"a":1,"x":"A coping saw has a thin, narrow blade that can turn corners. A tenon saw and a panel saw cut straight lines, and a hacksaw is for metals.","w":["A tenon saw has a stiff back and a wide blade, so it only cuts straight lines.","","A hacksaw is for metals, and its blade is not made for tight curves in timber.","A panel saw has a wide blade for long straight cuts; it cannot turn tight curves."]}
{"id":"gdt-030","s":"dt","t":"3.3.11","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a wasting process?","o":["Gluing two pieces of timber together","Steam-bending a strip of timber","Casting pewter in a mould","Drilling a hole in a piece of timber"],"a":3,"x":"Wasting removes material, as in sawing, drilling and planing. Gluing adds material, bending deforms it and casting reforms it.","w":["Gluing adds or joins material; nothing is removed, so it is not wasting.","Steam bending reshapes the timber without removing any, so it is not wasting.","Casting pours molten metal into a mould; that reforms material rather than removing it.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-031","s":"dt","t":"mat-tb","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a hardwood?","o":["Oak","Pine","Spruce","Larch"],"a":0,"x":"Oak is a hardwood from a broad-leaved tree. Pine, spruce and larch are softwoods from conifers.","w":["","Pine is a softwood from a conifer; 'soft' is about tree type, not how hard it feels.","Spruce is a softwood from a conifer, not a hardwood.","Larch is a softwood from a conifer, even though the timber is fairly tough."]}
{"id":"gdt-032","s":"dt","t":"mat-tb","lv":2,"q":"Which statement about MDF is correct?","o":["It is a natural hardwood","It is very water resistant, so it is ideal outdoors","It has no grain and a smooth surface that is good for painting","It is made by peeling thin veneers and gluing them"],"a":2,"x":"MDF is made from fine wood fibres and resin. It has no grain and a smooth surface, but it swells when it gets wet. Plywood is the board made from veneers.","w":["MDF is a manufactured board made from wood fibres, not a natural timber.","MDF swells when it gets wet, so it is a poor choice outdoors.","","That describes plywood; MDF is made from fine fibres and resin."]}
{"id":"gdt-033","s":"dt","t":"mat-tb","lv":2,"q":"Why are the layers of plywood glued with the grain of each layer at right angles to the next?","o":["So the board is weaker and easier to cut","So the board is strong in both directions and resists warping","So the board can be bent into tight curves","So the board weighs less than solid timber"],"a":1,"x":"Alternating the grain direction gives strength in more than one direction and reduces warping and splitting, unlike solid timber.","w":["Crossed grain makes the board stronger, not weaker.","","Crossed layers make the board stiffer, so they do not help it bend into tight curves.","Crossed layers are about strength and stability, not about saving weight."]}
{"id":"gdt-034","s":"dt","t":"mat-tb","lv":3,"q":"A school wants a garden bench that needs little maintenance. Which choice is best justified?","o":["MDF, because it has no grain and is cheap","Balsa, because it is light and easy to cut","Untreated pine, because softwood always lasts outdoors","Teak, because its natural oils resist rot"],"a":3,"x":"Teak is a hardwood with natural oils that resist moisture and rot. MDF swells in water, balsa is soft and weak, and untreated pine can rot outdoors.","w":["MDF swells and breaks down when wet, so it fails outdoors.","Balsa is very soft and weak, so a bench made from it would dent and break.","Untreated pine can rot outdoors; 'always lasts' is untrue.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-035","s":"dt","t":"nea-A","lv":1,"q":"Which activity belongs to the first part of the NEA, identifying and investigating design possibilities?","o":["Choosing a contextual challenge and researching users' needs","Testing the finished prototype against the specification","Making the final prototype","Producing final working drawings"],"a":0,"x":"The NEA starts with choosing one of AQA's contextual challenges and investigating the needs of users and the opportunities for design.","w":["","Testing the prototype comes later, in the evaluation part of the NEA.","Making the prototype comes after the research and design work.","Working drawings come in the design development part, not the investigation."]}
{"id":"gdt-036","s":"dt","t":"nea-B","lv":2,"q":"Which is the best specification point for a desk tidy?","o":["It should look attractive to the people who use it","It must be made to a good quality standard","It must hold at least six pens and be no taller than 120 mm","It should be easy and comfortable to use"],"a":2,"x":"A good specification point is measurable or testable, so you can check it at the evaluation stage. The other statements are vague.","w":["'Attractive' is a matter of opinion and cannot be measured or tested.","'Good quality standard' is vague and cannot be measured.","","'Easy and comfortable' is vague and cannot be checked with a measurement."]}
{"id":"gdt-037","s":"dt","t":"nea-C","lv":1,"q":"Which best shows good practice when generating design ideas?","o":["Drawing one idea in great detail","Sketching a range of ideas with notes on each","Copying an existing product exactly","Writing a long description with no drawings"],"a":1,"x":"The stage rewards a range of creative ideas communicated clearly, with notes explaining how each could meet the brief and specification. One idea is not a range.","w":["One detailed idea gives no range, so it limits creativity and choice.","","Copying is not original; your ideas should be your own and meet the brief.","Ideas must be shown visually, so sketches are needed, not just words."]}
{"id":"gdt-038","s":"dt","t":"nea-D","lv":2,"q":"A student makes a card mock-up of a chosen design to check its size and shape. Which NEA stage is this?","o":["Identifying and investigating design possibilities","Writing the design brief","Analysing and evaluating","Developing design ideas"],"a":3,"x":"Modelling and testing a chosen idea, then improving it, is part of developing design ideas, before the final prototype is made.","w":["That is early research into needs and existing products, not modelling a chosen idea.","The brief is written near the start, long before any mock-up is made.","Evaluation runs throughout, but making and testing a mock-up is developing the idea.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-039","s":"dt","t":"nea-E","lv":2,"q":"Which is the safest way to prepare to use a pillar drill?","o":["Clamp the work down, tie back long hair and wear eye protection","Hold the work with your hand so you can feel it","Wear thick gloves to protect your hands","Remove the guard so you can see the drill bit clearly"],"a":0,"x":"Work should be clamped. Loose clothing, long hair and gloves can be caught by rotating parts. Guards must stay in place and eye protection should be worn.","w":["","Holding the work by hand lets it spin and injure you; clamp it instead.","Gloves can be caught by the spinning drill and pull your hand in.","Guards protect you; removing one makes injury more likely."]}
{"id":"gdt-040","s":"dt","t":"nea-F","lv":2,"q":"Which is the best way to evaluate your finished prototype?","o":["List only the things that went well","Change the specification to match the prototype","Test it against each point in your specification","Ask one friend whether they like it"],"a":2,"x":"AQA rewards testing every aspect of the prototype against the brief and specification, backed by feedback from users. The results then show what to improve.","w":["Listing only positives is biased; you need to find weaknesses to improve.","Changing the specification hides failures; test against the original one.","","One opinion is too narrow; test against the spec and gather wider feedback."]}
{"id":"gdt-041","s":"dt","t":"3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A car factory uses robots to weld body panels. Which is a real advantage for the company?","o":["Robots need no maintenance, so repair costs fall to zero","Robots need no programming once they are bought","Robots can work all day and night with the same accuracy","Robots can decide how to redesign the car by themselves"],"a":2,"x":"Automation lets robots repeat a task for long periods at the same accuracy and speed. The trap is thinking robots are free to run: they still need programming, maintenance and a high set-up cost.","w":["Robots still wear out and need servicing, so repair costs do not fall to zero.","Robots must be programmed and reprogrammed for each task, so this is a myth about automation.","","Robots only follow their programs. Designers make design decisions, not the welding robots."]}
{"id":"gdt-042","s":"dt","t":"3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A student invents a bike light but has no money to make it. How could crowdfunding help?","o":["Many people each give a small sum, often for a reward like an early light","A bank lends the full amount and the student pays it back with interest","The government pays for production and then owns the design","A large company buys the idea and makes the light for the student"],"a":0,"x":"Crowdfunding raises money from many people online, often in return for a reward or early product. It also shows if there is demand. The trap is confusing it with a bank loan or a company buying the idea.","w":["","This describes a bank loan, not crowdfunding, which collects small sums from many backers.","Crowdfunding does not rely on the government, and backers do not take over the design.","This is selling or licensing an idea, not crowdfunding, where the inventor keeps control."]}
{"id":"gdt-043","s":"dt","t":"3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A factory replaces many assembly workers with robots. Which change in jobs is most likely?","o":["No people are needed in the factory at all","More unskilled assembly jobs, as robots must be fed by hand","Every worker is paid more because robots are costly","Fewer repetitive manual jobs, but more skilled jobs maintaining robots"],"a":3,"x":"Automation often cuts repetitive manual jobs but creates skilled jobs such as technicians and programmers. The trap is thinking all jobs vanish. People are still needed to set up, check and maintain the machines.","w":["Robots still need people to program, supervise and repair them, so jobs do not vanish completely.","Robots take over repeated assembly tasks, so unskilled assembly jobs usually fall, not rise.","Buying robots does not mean pay rises for all, and many workers lose their roles.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-044","s":"dt","t":"3.1.1","lv":3,"q":"A firm stops selling batteries for its 3-year-old tablets, so owners buy new ones. What is the most likely environmental effect?","o":["Less waste, because new tablets use no extra materials","More electronic waste, and more resources and energy used to make replacements","No change, because old tablets are always repaired and reused","Fewer raw materials mined, because fewer tablets are bought"],"a":1,"x":"This is planned obsolescence: the product's life is cut short, so more tablets are thrown away and more are made. That uses more materials and energy. The trap is ignoring the cost of making the replacements.","w":["Every new tablet needs new materials and energy to make, so this ignores manufacturing impact.","","With no replacement batteries sold, many old tablets cannot be repaired and are thrown away.","Owners buy more tablets, not fewer, so more raw materials are used."]}
{"id":"gdt-045","s":"dt","t":"3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Why are coal, oil and natural gas called non-renewable energy sources?","o":["They cannot be burned to release heat energy","They only work when the weather is dry and sunny","They take millions of years to form and are used up faster than they form","They are made in nuclear reactors from uranium"],"a":2,"x":"Fossil fuels formed from ancient plants and animals over millions of years. We burn them much faster than nature makes them, so they will run out. The trap is linking non-renewable to weather or nuclear fuel.","w":["Fossil fuels burn very well, and non-renewable means their supply will run out.","Depending on sunny weather describes solar power, not fossil fuels.","","Uranium is nuclear fuel, so this mixes up nuclear power with fossil fuels."]}
{"id":"gdt-046","s":"dt","t":"3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Which is an advantage of generating electricity in a nuclear power station?","o":["It produces no waste of any kind","A small amount of fuel gives a very large amount of energy, with low CO₂ released","Its uranium fuel is a renewable resource","It can be switched on and off in seconds to match demand"],"a":1,"x":"Nuclear fission releases huge energy from a little uranium and gives out almost no CO₂ while running. The trap is forgetting it still makes dangerous radioactive waste, and its fuel is not renewable.","w":["Nuclear stations make radioactive waste that must be stored safely for a very long time.","","Uranium is mined and will run out, so low CO₂ does not make it renewable.","Nuclear reactors change output slowly, so they cannot be switched on and off in seconds."]}
{"id":"gdt-047","s":"dt","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A country gets much of its electricity from wind and solar. Why does it also need ways of storing energy?","o":["Wind and solar produce too much heat to use directly","Stored energy is the only way to make renewable electricity cheaper than fossil fuels","Wind turbines and solar panels cannot make any electricity without a battery","Their output changes with the weather and may not match demand"],"a":3,"x":"Wind and sun are not constant, so output can be high when demand is low and low when demand is high. Storage, such as pumped storage or batteries, saves spare energy for later. The trap is thinking they cannot generate at all.","w":["Heat is not the problem; the issue is that their output varies with the weather.","Storage is used to balance supply and demand, not to decide cost against fossil fuels.","Turbines and panels generate electricity directly and only need storage to cover times of low output.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-048","s":"dt","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A designer chooses a lithium-ion battery for a phone, not a single-use alkaline battery. What is the best reason?","o":["It can be recharged many times and stores lots of energy for its mass","It makes electricity by burning a fuel inside the phone","It is not a chemical cell, so it never wears out","It stores less energy, which makes the phone lighter"],"a":0,"x":"Lithium-ion cells are rechargeable and have high energy density, so a small, light cell can power a phone. Alkaline cells are usually single-use. The trap is thinking all batteries are the same or never wear out.","w":["","Batteries store energy chemically and do not burn fuel, unlike fossil fuel power stations.","Lithium-ion cells are chemical cells and do wear out after many charge cycles.","Phones need more stored energy, not less, and lithium-ion stores more energy per kilogram."]}
{"id":"gdt-049","s":"dt","t":"3.1.2","lv":3,"q":"A pumped storage scheme uses 640 MWh of electricity to pump water uphill. Its overall efficiency is 75%. How much electricity does it give back when the water is released?","o":["160 MWh","640 MWh","480 MWh","853 MWh"],"a":2,"x":"Efficiency 75% means 75% of the input is returned: $640 \\times 0.75 = 480$ MWh. The rest is lost, mainly as heat. The trap is giving the energy lost, or dividing by 0.75.","w":["This is the energy lost (25% of 640), not the energy returned.","This assumes 100% efficiency, but some energy is always lost as heat and friction.","","This divides 640 by 0.75 instead of multiplying, giving more out than was put in."]}
{"id":"gdt-050","s":"dt","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Nitinol is a shape memory alloy. Which property makes it a smart material?","o":["It changes colour when its temperature rises","It returns to its original shape when it is heated","It makes a voltage when it is squashed","It gets darker when UV light shines on it"],"a":1,"x":"A shape memory alloy can be bent, then goes back to its remembered shape when heated. Colour change with heat is thermochromic, and darkening in UV light is photochromic. Making a voltage when squashed is piezoelectric.","w":["Colour change with heat is thermochromic pigment, not a shape memory alloy, so this mixes up two smart materials.","","Making a voltage when squashed is a piezoelectric effect, not a shape memory property.","Darkening in UV light is photochromic behaviour, so this mixes up two smart materials."]}
{"id":"gdt-051","s":"dt","t":"3.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A walker wants a jacket that keeps rain out but lets sweat vapour escape. Which technical textile suits this best?","o":["Kevlar, because it resists cuts and is very strong","Nomex, because it resists flames and high heat","A microencapsulated fabric, because it releases scent when rubbed","Gore-Tex, because its fine membrane stops rain but lets vapour through"],"a":3,"x":"Gore-Tex has a microporous membrane. Its pores are too small for liquid water but big enough for water vapour. The trap is picking a high-performance textile chosen for strength, flame resistance or scent instead.","w":["Kevlar is chosen for strength and cut resistance, not for keeping rain out while letting sweat escape.","Nomex is flame resistant, used for firefighter clothing, and it is not a breathable waterproof fabric.","Microencapsulation holds scents or chemicals in tiny capsules, which does nothing for waterproofing or breathability.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-052","s":"dt","t":"3.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A boat hull is made from glass reinforced plastic (GRP). Which statement best explains how the two parts of GRP work together?","o":["The glass fibres give strength and the resin holds them in place and sets the shape","The resin gives all the strength and the glass fibres only add colour","The glass fibres melt into the resin to make one pure material","The resin carries electric current and the glass fibres stop it leaking"],"a":0,"x":"In a composite, the fibres carry the loads and give strength. The resin binds the fibres, holds the shape and passes force between them. Mixing up the jobs of the fibre and the matrix is a common slip.","w":["","This reverses the roles. The fibres give the strength, and the resin is the matrix that binds them.","The fibres do not melt. A composite keeps its two materials separate, so it is not a single pure material.","GRP is not made to conduct electricity. This confuses composites with conductive materials."]}
{"id":"gdt-053","s":"dt","t":"3.1.3","lv":3,"q":"A student suggests four uses for smart and technical materials. Which suggestion uses the material for a property it really has?","o":["Photochromic pigment on a kettle label to show when the water is hot","A shape memory alloy in a pen clip so that it darkens in sunlight","Thermochromic pigment on a baby bath strip to show when the water is too hot","Kevlar fibres in a jacket lining so that it changes colour with temperature"],"a":2,"x":"Thermochromic pigment changes colour with temperature, so it can warn that bath water is too hot. Photochromic pigment reacts to UV light, not heat. Shape memory alloys change shape, not colour. Kevlar is valued for strength.","w":["Photochromic pigment reacts to UV light, not heat. The student has confused it with thermochromic pigment.","Shape memory alloys return to a set shape when heated and do not darken in sunlight. That is photochromic behaviour.","","Kevlar is a strong, cut-resistant fibre and does not change colour. The student has confused it with thermochromic materials."]}
{"id":"gdt-054","s":"dt","t":"3.1.4","lv":1,"q":"A night-time reading lamp has a light-dependent resistor (LDR), a microcontroller and an LED. Which part is an input device?","o":["The LED","The LDR","The microcontroller","The battery"],"a":1,"x":"The LDR senses how bright it is and sends a signal in, so it is the input. The LED is the output. The microcontroller is the process. A battery only supplies power.","w":["The LED gives out light, so it is an output, not an input.","","The microcontroller makes decisions, so it is the process stage, not an input.","A battery supplies energy but senses nothing, so it is not an input device."]}
{"id":"gdt-056","s":"dt","t":"3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"A greenhouse watering system has a soil moisture sensor, a microcontroller and a water pump. Which row labels the parts correctly as input, process and output?","o":["Input: pump; Process: sensor; Output: microcontroller","Input: microcontroller; Process: sensor; Output: pump","Input: sensor; Process: pump; Output: microcontroller","Input: sensor; Process: microcontroller; Output: pump"],"a":3,"x":"The sensor detects dryness (input). The microcontroller decides when to water (process). The pump moves the water (output). Check each part by asking what it does, not where it sits.","w":["This reverses the whole system. The pump does the job, so it is the output, not the input.","The sensor detects, so it is the input, and the microcontroller is the process. These two are swapped.","The pump acts on the decision, so it is the output, and the microcontroller is the process.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-057","s":"dt","t":"3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"A fridge door alarm has a switch, a microcontroller and a buzzer. If the door stays open for 60 seconds, the buzzer sounds. Which of these is the process?","o":["The switch detecting that the door has opened","The buzzer making a sound","The microcontroller timing 60 seconds before the buzzer","The battery supplying power to the circuit"],"a":2,"x":"The process is the decision-making stage. The microcontroller counts the time and decides when to switch the buzzer on. The switch is the input and the buzzer is the output.","w":["Detecting the door opening is the input's job; mixing up sensing with deciding is the slip.","The buzzer is the output because it produces the sound the user hears.","","A battery is the power supply; it makes no decisions, so it is not the process."]}
{"id":"gdt-058","s":"dt","t":"3.1.4","lv":3,"q":"A night light uses an LDR, a microcontroller and an LED. A student adds a feature: a buzzer sounds if someone walks past in the dark. Which parts must be added to the system?","o":["A buzzer as an input and a PIR sensor as an output","A PIR sensor as an input and a buzzer as an output","A PIR sensor and a buzzer, both as inputs","A second microcontroller as an input and a PIR sensor as an output"],"a":1,"x":"A PIR sensor detects movement, so it is an input. A buzzer makes a sound, so it is an output. The existing microcontroller can process both. The trap is swapping input and output roles.","w":["This swaps the roles: a buzzer makes sound so it is an output, and a PIR detects movement so it is an input.","","The buzzer is not an input because it does not sense anything; it gives out sound.","A second microcontroller is not needed and is a process part, and a PIR is an input, not an output."]}
{"id":"gdt-059","s":"dt","t":"3.1.5","lv":1,"q":"A sewing machine needle moves up and down in a straight line, over and over again. What type of movement is this?","o":["Linear","Reciprocating","Oscillating","Rotary"],"a":1,"x":"Reciprocating movement is back-and-forth in a straight line. Linear movement goes one way only. Oscillating movement swings along an arc. Rotary movement goes round in a circle.","w":["Linear movement goes one way in a straight line, but the needle keeps going back and forth.","","Oscillating movement swings back and forth along a curved arc, but the needle moves in a straight line.","Rotary movement turns round in a circle, but the needle moves up and down without turning."]}
{"id":"gdt-060","s":"dt","t":"3.1.5","lv":2,"q":"A reverse motion linkage has a bar with a fixed pivot. The input is 50 mm from the pivot. The output is 100 mm from it, on the other side. The input moves up 20 mm. What does the output do?","o":["Moves up 40 mm","Moves down 20 mm","Moves down 10 mm","Moves down 40 mm"],"a":3,"x":"A reverse motion linkage makes the output move the opposite way. The output is twice as far from the pivot, so it moves twice as far: 20 × 2 = 40 mm down. The trap is ignoring the distances from the pivot.","w":["The size is right, but a reverse motion linkage sends the output the opposite way, so it moves down.","The direction is right, but this ignores that the output is twice as far from the pivot.","The ratio is inverted, because the longer arm moves further, so the output moves more than the input.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-061","s":"dt","t":"3.1.5","lv":2,"q":"A follower rises slowly during most of one turn of a cam, then drops suddenly back to its start position. Which cam gives this movement?","o":["Snail (drop) cam","Eccentric (circular) cam","Pear cam","Circular cam with the shaft at its centre"],"a":0,"x":"A snail (drop) cam has a gradual rise, then a sharp vertical edge where the follower drops. An eccentric cam rises and falls smoothly at an even rate. A pear cam gives a rest (dwell) on its round part.","w":["","An eccentric cam has an off-centre shaft, so it raises and lowers the follower smoothly with no sudden drop.","A pear cam gives a long rest (dwell) then a short rise and fall, not a slow rise and sudden drop.","With the shaft at its centre, a circular cam gives no up-and-down movement of the follower at all."]}
{"id":"gdt-062","s":"dt","t":"3.1.5","lv":3,"q":"A motor turns a 25 mm diameter pulley at 900 rpm. A crossed belt joins it to a 75 mm diameter pulley. What is the speed and direction of the driven pulley compared with the motor?","o":["300 rpm, same direction","2700 rpm, opposite direction","300 rpm, opposite direction","2700 rpm, same direction"],"a":2,"x":"Speed = 900 × 25 ÷ 75 = 300 rpm. The larger driven pulley turns slower. A crossed belt reverses the direction, while an open belt keeps it the same. The traps are inverting the ratio and forgetting the crossed belt.","w":["The speed is right, but a crossed belt reverses the direction; only an open belt keeps it the same.","The ratio is inverted, as 900 × 75 ÷ 25 makes the larger driven pulley turn faster instead of slower.","","The ratio is inverted so the speed is too high, and the crossed belt reverses the direction too."]}
{"id":"gdt-063","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.1","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a thermosetting polymer?","o":["Acrylic","High-density polyethylene (HDPE)","Epoxy resin","Polyvinyl chloride (PVC)"],"a":2,"x":"Thermosetting polymers set hard once and cannot be reshaped by heating. Epoxy resin is one. Acrylic, HDPE and PVC are thermoforming polymers, which soften again when heated. The trap is assuming hard, rigid plastics must be thermosetting.","w":["Acrylic softens when heated and can be bent again, so it is thermoforming even though it is hard and clear.","HDPE can be melted and reshaped many times, so it is thermoforming, not thermosetting.","","PVC softens on heating and can be reshaped, so it is thermoforming; being rigid does not make it thermosetting."]}
{"id":"gdt-064","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.1","lv":1,"q":"Balsa wood is very soft and light. Which timber category does it belong to?","o":["Softwood","Hardwood","Manufactured board","Composite"],"a":1,"x":"Hardwood and softwood describe the type of tree, not how hard the wood feels. Balsa comes from a broad-leaved tree, so it is a hardwood. The trap is judging by softness.","w":["Softwoods come from cone-bearing trees like pine, but balsa comes from a broad-leaved tree, so judging by feel is the slip.","","Manufactured boards are made from bonded layers, chips or fibres, but balsa is a natural solid timber.","A composite combines two different materials, but balsa is a single natural timber."]}
{"id":"gdt-065","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.1","lv":2,"q":"Brass is made by mixing copper and zinc. Which description of brass is correct?","o":["A pure ferrous metal","A ferrous alloy","A pure non-ferrous metal","A non-ferrous alloy"],"a":3,"x":"An alloy is a mixture of metals. Brass is copper and zinc, with no iron, so it is non-ferrous. That makes it a non-ferrous alloy. The trap is thinking it is a pure metal.","w":["Brass is a mixture of two metals, so it is not pure, and it contains no iron, so it is not ferrous.","Ferrous alloys such as steel contain iron, but brass has only copper and zinc, so it is not ferrous.","Brass is a mixture of copper and zinc, so it is an alloy rather than a pure metal.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-066","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.1","lv":2,"q":"A shirt fabric is woven from yarn. Each yarn was made by spinning polyester fibres and cotton fibres together. What type of fabric is this?","o":["A blended fabric","A synthetic fabric","A natural fabric","A mixed fabric"],"a":0,"x":"Different fibres spun together into one yarn make a blended fabric. A mixed fabric uses different yarns, each of one fibre, woven together. Because cotton and polyester are both present, it is neither fully natural nor fully synthetic.","w":["","Cotton is a natural fibre, so the fabric is not fully synthetic; ignoring the cotton is the slip.","Polyester is a synthetic fibre, so the fabric is not fully natural; ignoring the polyester is the slip.","Mixed fabrics weave separate yarns of different fibres, but here the fibres were spun together first, so it is blended."]}
{"id":"gdt-067","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.1","lv":3,"q":"A plywood sheet is less likely to split along the grain than a solid pine plank of the same thickness. Why?","o":["All its veneers are glued with the grain in the same direction","It is made only from hardwood, which never splits","Each veneer has its grain at right angles to the next one","Its wood fibres are broken down and pressed with resin, so it has no grain"],"a":2,"x":"Plywood is made of thin veneers glued with the grain of each layer at right angles to the next. This spreads strength in both directions and resists splitting. Describing fibres pressed with resin is MDF, not plywood.","w":["Layers with grain all in one direction would split like solid timber; plywood alternates the grain direction.","Plywood can be made from softwood or hardwood, and hardwood can still split; strength comes from the layer pattern.","","This describes MDF, made from wood fibres and resin, whereas plywood is made from veneers, so mixing up boards is the slip."]}
{"id":"gdt-068","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.2","lv":1,"q":"A saucepan base must heat food quickly. Which property should the base material have?","o":["Low thermal conductivity","High thermal conductivity","High fusibility","High absorbency"],"a":1,"x":"High thermal conductivity lets heat pass through the base quickly into the food. Low conductivity makes a good insulator, which suits a handle. High fusibility would let the pan melt.","w":["Low thermal conductivity slows heat flow, so this mixes up a conductor for the base with an insulator for the handle.","","High fusibility means melting easily, so the slip is confusing melting with transferring heat.","Absorbency is soaking up liquids, so the slip is picking a property that has nothing to do with heat."]}
{"id":"gdt-069","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.2","lv":1,"q":"Solder is melted to join wires on a circuit board. Which property must solder have?","o":["High hardness","High density","High absorbency","High fusibility"],"a":3,"x":"Fusibility is how easily a material melts and fuses. Solder must melt easily at a low temperature so it does not damage components. Hardness and density do not help it melt.","w":["Hardness is resistance to scratching or denting, so it does not make solder melt easily.","Density is mass per unit volume, so it tells you nothing about how easily solder melts.","Absorbency is how much liquid a material soaks up, which is unrelated to melting solder.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-070","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.2","lv":2,"q":"The core of an electrical cable is made from copper. Which pair of properties makes copper suitable for this job?","o":["Electrical insulator and ductile","Good electrical conductor and brittle","Good electrical conductor and ductile","Electrical insulator and tough"],"a":2,"x":"The core must carry current, so it needs high electrical conductivity. It is drawn into thin wire, so it must be ductile. Insulation belongs to the plastic outer sheath, not the core.","w":["An insulator cannot carry current, so this mixes up the copper core with the plastic sheath.","Conductivity is right, but a brittle wire would snap when drawn out or bent.","","An insulator cannot carry current, so this describes the outer sheath rather than the core."]}
{"id":"gdt-071","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.2","lv":2,"q":"A ceramic mug is very hard to scratch but shatters when dropped on a tiled floor. Which description fits the ceramic best?","o":["Hard and brittle","Soft and tough","Hard and tough","Soft and malleable"],"a":0,"x":"Ceramic resists scratching, so it is hard. It cracks under sudden impact, so it is brittle with low toughness. The trap is thinking hard means tough. They are different properties.","w":["","The mug resists scratching so it is not soft, and it shatters so it is not tough.","A tough mug would survive the drop, so the slip is assuming hard and tough mean the same.","The mug resists scratching so it is not soft, and it shatters rather than changing shape."]}
{"id":"gdt-072","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.2","lv":3,"q":"Which row correctly links each process to the property the metal needs?","o":["Pressing a car door panel: ductility. Drawing copper into wire: malleability.","Pressing a car door panel: malleability. Drawing copper into wire: ductility.","Pressing a car door panel: elasticity. Drawing copper into wire: malleability.","Pressing a car door panel: hardness. Drawing copper into wire: toughness."],"a":1,"x":"Pressing a panel into shape needs malleability. Drawing metal into wire needs ductility. The common trap is swapping the two words. Elasticity would make the panel spring back instead of keeping its new shape.","w":["The two properties are swapped, as ductility is for drawing wire and malleability is for pressing into shape.","","Elasticity would make the panel spring back, and wire drawing needs ductility, not malleability.","Hardness resists shaping and toughness is impact resistance, so neither lets the metal be formed."]}
{"id":"gdt-073","s":"dt","t":"3.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is an ethical factor a designer might consider when choosing a material?","o":["Whether it is stiff enough for the job","Whether it is sold in standard sizes","Whether its colour suits the brand","Whether the workers who made it were treated fairly"],"a":3,"x":"Ethical factors are about right and wrong in how a material is made or sourced, such as fair pay, safe working conditions and no child labour. Stiffness, sizes and colour are not ethical issues.","w":["Stiffness is a functional property, not an ethical issue. Confusing function with ethics is the slip.","Standard sizes relate to availability and cost, not to fairness or right and wrong.","Colour is an aesthetic factor. It does not involve the treatment of workers or animals.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-074","s":"dt","t":"3.2.1","lv":1,"q":"A company sells gift boxes in many countries. It avoids one colour because in one country it is linked to mourning. Which type of factor is this?","o":["Ethical","Cultural","Environmental","Availability"],"a":1,"x":"Cultural factors cover the beliefs, customs and symbols of different groups. A colour with a different meaning in another country is a cultural issue. Do not confuse it with ethics, which is about fair treatment.","w":["Ethical factors cover things like fair pay and animal welfare. Colour meanings are about customs, not fairness.","","Environmental factors cover pollution, waste and resources. A colour's meaning has nothing to do with these.","Availability is about whether a material can be bought. The problem here is the colour's meaning, not supply."]}
{"id":"gdt-075","s":"dt","t":"3.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Acrylic sheet costs £12 per sheet. The supplier gives 10% off the whole order if 20 or more sheets are bought. A firm needs 25 sheets. What is the total cost?","o":["£270","£300","£30","£288"],"a":0,"x":"Full price is 25 × £12 = £300. The discount is 10% of £300 = £30. So the cost is £300 − £30 = £270. The trap is giving the saving, or forgetting the bulk discount.","w":["","£300 is the price with no discount. The order is over 20 sheets, so the bulk discount applies.","£30 is only the saving. The question asks for the total cost after the saving is taken off.","£288 takes the price of one sheet off, not 10% of the whole order."]}
{"id":"gdt-076","s":"dt","t":"3.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A furniture maker chooses oak grown 50 miles away instead of oak shipped from another continent. What is the main environmental reason for this choice?","o":["It is always cheaper per cubic metre","It makes the finished table stronger","Shorter transport gives a lower carbon footprint","It guarantees the wood is FSC certified"],"a":2,"x":"Sourcing locally means fewer transport miles, so less fuel burned and less CO₂. Local timber is not always cheaper, is not stronger, and is not automatically FSC certified.","w":["Local timber is not always cheaper. Cost is not an environmental reason anyway.","Where oak grows does not change its strength. That depends on the wood itself.","","Certification is separate from distance. Local wood can still come from poorly managed forests."]}
{"id":"gdt-077","s":"dt","t":"3.2.1","lv":3,"q":"A council wants a park bench that lasts 20 years outdoors with little upkeep and uses waste material. Which material choice best meets all of this?","o":["Untreated pine","Unprotected mild steel","Chipboard","Recycled plastic lumber"],"a":3,"x":"Recycled plastic lumber does not rot or rust, needs little upkeep and reuses waste plastic. Untreated pine rots, unprotected mild steel rusts, and chipboard swells when wet. The trap is choosing on cheapness alone.","w":["Untreated pine is cheap but rots outdoors and needs regular treatment. It is also not made from waste.","Mild steel without a coating rusts outdoors, so it would need regular upkeep to last 20 years.","Chipboard swells and breaks down when it gets wet. It is not suitable outdoors for 20 years.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-078","s":"dt","t":"3.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Two steel plates are joined by one bolt. The plates are pulled in opposite directions so they try to slide past each other. Which force tries to cut across the bolt?","o":["Tension","Compression","Shear","Torsion"],"a":2,"x":"Shear is a sliding force that acts across a material and tries to cut it. The plates pull sideways on the bolt. The trap is picking tension just because the plates are being pulled.","w":["Tension stretches along the length. Here the force acts across the bolt, so this confuses pulling with cutting.","Compression squashes a material along its length. Nothing here is pushing the bolt together end to end.","","Torsion is twisting about an axis. The plates slide sideways and do not twist the bolt."]}
{"id":"gdt-079","s":"dt","t":"3.2.2","lv":1,"q":"A screwdriver is turned to drive in a tight screw. Which force mainly acts on the shaft of the screwdriver?","o":["Torsion","Bending","Tension","Compression"],"a":0,"x":"Torsion is a twisting force. Turning the handle twists the shaft about its own length. The trap is thinking any force that turns something must be bending.","w":["","Bending happens when a sideways load makes a material curve. The shaft stays straight and twists.","Tension is a pulling force along the length. Turning the handle does not stretch the shaft.","Compression is a squashing force. Pushing down on the handle adds a little, but the main effect is twisting."]}
{"id":"gdt-080","s":"dt","t":"3.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A tent has one upright pole holding up the canvas. Ropes run from the canvas to pegs in the ground. Which pair correctly gives the forces in the pole and in a rope?","o":["Pole in tension, rope in compression","Both the pole and the rope in tension","Pole in torsion, rope in shear","Pole in compression, rope in tension"],"a":3,"x":"The canvas pushes down on the pole, so it is squashed (compression). The ropes are pulled tight between canvas and pegs, so they are stretched (tension). The trap is swapping the two.","w":["Reversed: the pole is pushed down and squashed, and the rope is pulled tight.","The rope is in tension but the pole is pushed down on, so it is in compression, not tension.","Neither is being twisted or cut across. The pole is squashed and the rope is stretched.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-081","s":"dt","t":"3.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A wooden garden gate is a rectangular frame. Over time it sags and its corners change shape. Which change would best stop this happening?","o":["Add a second horizontal rail near the top","Fix a diagonal brace between opposite corners","Use longer screws in the hinges","Paint the frame with wood preservative"],"a":1,"x":"A diagonal brace makes triangles, and a triangle cannot change shape without changing side lengths. This stiffens the frame. The trap is adding more rectangles, which can still lean over.","w":["This only makes another rectangle, which can still lean over. It does not triangulate the frame.","","Longer screws may hold the hinges better but do not stop the frame racking out of shape.","Preservative protects against rot and does not make the frame stiffer."]}
{"id":"gdt-082","s":"dt","t":"3.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A steel I-beam and a solid rectangular steel bar have the same length and the same cross-sectional area. Both are loaded in the middle. Why is the I-beam stiffer in bending?","o":["It contains more steel than the solid bar","The thin middle part carries all the compression","Most steel is in the top and bottom flanges, far from the centre","The I shape changes the bending force into a shear force"],"a":2,"x":"Bending stresses are greatest at the top and bottom surfaces. The I-beam puts most of its material there, in the flanges, so it resists bending better. The trap is thinking it has more material.","w":["The cross-sectional area is the same, so the amount of steel per metre is the same.","The middle web does not take all the compression. The flanges carry most of the bending stress.","","The shape does not change the type of force. It is stiffer because material is placed where it resists bending best."]}
{"id":"gdt-083","s":"dt","t":"3.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a problem with a product's SOCIAL footprint, rather than its ecological footprint?","o":["Trees are cut down to make the wooden parts","Factory chimneys release carbon dioxide into the air","Workers in the factory are paid very low wages for long hours","Waste plastic from the product ends up in the sea"],"a":2,"x":"Social footprint is about the people who make a product, such as pay, hours and safety. Deforestation, air pollution and plastic waste are ecological harms to the environment. Do not mix the two up.","w":["Cutting down trees is deforestation. That is an ecological harm, not a harm to workers.","Carbon dioxide emissions add to global warming. This is an ecological issue, not a working-conditions one.","","Plastic in the sea is pollution. It harms the environment, so it is ecological, not social."]}
{"id":"gdt-084","s":"dt","t":"3.2.3","lv":1,"q":"A shop sells a Fairtrade-marked cotton T-shirt. What is the main aim of the Fairtrade mark?","o":["Farmers and workers get a fair price and better working conditions","The T-shirt is always cheaper for the customer to buy","The T-shirt is made only from materials grown in the UK","The T-shirt can be fully recycled at the end of its life"],"a":0,"x":"Fairtrade is a social aim. It makes sure producers in poorer countries get a fair price and safer, fairer working conditions. It does not guarantee a low price, UK origin or recyclability.","w":["","Fairtrade often costs a little more. The mark is about fair pay for producers, not low prices for customers.","Fairtrade usually helps producers in developing countries. It has nothing to do with UK-grown materials.","Recyclability is an ecological point. Fairtrade is about fair pay and conditions for people."]}
{"id":"gdt-085","s":"dt","t":"3.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A drinks company keeps its bottle the same size, but redesigns it to use 20% less plastic. Which of the six Rs does this show?","o":["Refuse","Reuse","Recycle","Reduce"],"a":3,"x":"Reduce means using less material or energy. Making the bottle with 20% less plastic does exactly this. Reuse and Recycle happen after use, and Refuse means declining a product or material.","w":["Refuse means choosing not to use a harmful material at all. The company still uses plastic, just less.","Reuse means using the same bottle again. The new bottle is not being used a second time.","Recycle means reprocessing used material. Here less new plastic is used, so it is not recycling.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-086","s":"dt","t":"3.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A company wants to cut the carbon footprint of delivering its products to shops overseas. Which change would help most?","o":["Sending the goods by air freight so they arrive faster","Sending the goods by sea freight instead of air freight","Adding extra packaging to protect the goods on the way","Using a factory powered by burning coal"],"a":1,"x":"Air freight produces far more carbon dioxide per tonne of goods than sea freight. Switching to sea lowers the carbon footprint. Faster delivery does not mean greener delivery.","w":["Air freight gives off much more CO₂ than sea freight. Speed is not the same as lower impact.","","Extra packaging uses more material and adds weight. It raises the footprint rather than cutting it.","Burning coal releases a lot of CO₂. It would increase the carbon footprint, not cut it."]}
{"id":"gdt-087","s":"dt","t":"3.2.3","lv":3,"q":"Why is reusing a glass jar to store food usually better for the environment than recycling it?","o":["Reusing makes the glass stronger each time","Recycling glass uses no energy at all","Reusing needs no energy to melt and reshape the glass","Glass cannot be recycled into new products"],"a":2,"x":"Reuse keeps the jar as it is, so no energy is needed to collect, melt and remake it. Recycling is still useful, but it uses energy. The trap is thinking recycling is always the best choice.","w":["Reusing does not change the strength of the glass. It is just used again as it is.","Recycling needs energy to collect, melt and reform the glass, so this is wrong.","","Glass can be recycled many times. The statement is false, so it cannot explain the difference."]}
{"id":"gdt-088","s":"dt","t":"3.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which raw material is most polyester fabric made from?","o":["Wood pulp","Crude oil","Cotton plants","Sheep's fleece"],"a":1,"x":"Polyester is a synthetic fibre made from crude oil. Wood pulp, cotton and fleece are natural or plant-based sources, so choosing them confuses synthetic fibres with natural ones.","w":["Wood pulp is used for paper and viscose, not polyester; this mixes up regenerated and synthetic fibres.","","Cotton is a natural fibre grown as a plant; polyester is synthetic, so this mixes up natural and synthetic.","Fleece from sheep gives wool, a natural animal fibre, not a synthetic one like polyester."]}
{"id":"gdt-089","s":"dt","t":"3.2.4","lv":1,"q":"A cup is made from PLA, a bio-based polymer. Which raw material is PLA usually made from?","o":["Crude oil","Wood pulp","Bauxite ore","Corn starch"],"a":3,"x":"PLA is made from plant starch or sugars, such as corn starch. Most other polymers come from crude oil, which is the trap here.","w":["Crude oil is the source of most polymers, but PLA is bio-based so it comes from plants.","Wood pulp is used for paper, board and viscose, not PLA.","Bauxite is the ore used to extract aluminium, not a polymer source.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-090","s":"dt","t":"3.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A joiner buys kiln-seasoned oak instead of air-seasoned oak. What is the main benefit of kiln seasoning?","o":["It dries faster, to a controlled moisture level","It uses no energy, so it costs less","It turns the oak into a different kind of timber","It makes the timber heavier and denser"],"a":0,"x":"Kiln seasoning dries timber quickly to a set moisture content, so it is less likely to warp or split. The trap is thinking it is cheaper because air drying is natural.","w":["","Kilns use heat and energy; it is air seasoning that uses little energy, so this swaps the two.","Seasoning only removes water; it cannot change a hardwood into a softwood or another timber.","Removing water makes timber lighter, not heavier, so this reverses the effect."]}
{"id":"gdt-091","s":"dt","t":"3.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Why is recycling aluminium drinks cans better for the environment than making new aluminium from bauxite ore?","o":["Recycled aluminium is stronger than new aluminium","Aluminium can only be recycled once, so it is rarely wasted","It needs far less energy and avoids extra mining","Cans are ferrous, so magnets make them easy to sort"],"a":2,"x":"Melting used aluminium needs much less energy than extracting it from ore, and it cuts mining. The traps are thinking aluminium is magnetic or that recycling weakens it.","w":["Recycled aluminium has the same properties as new aluminium; it is not made stronger.","Aluminium can be recycled many times without losing quality, so this is wrong.","","Aluminium is non-ferrous, so magnets do not attract it; this mixes up ferrous and non-ferrous metals."]}
{"id":"gdt-092","s":"dt","t":"3.2.4","lv":3,"q":"A top is labelled viscose. Which statement about viscose is correct?","o":["It is made from crude oil by cracking and polymerisation","It is made from cellulose in wood pulp, chemically processed into fibres","It is spun from threads made by silkworms","It is made by melting and respinning old polyester bottles"],"a":1,"x":"Viscose is a regenerated fibre. Natural cellulose from wood pulp is chemically processed and spun into fibres. It is not fully natural and not made from crude oil.","w":["This describes synthetic fibres such as polyester; viscose starts from a plant material, not oil.","","Silkworms make silk, a natural animal fibre; this mixes up natural and regenerated fibres.","Respinning bottles gives recycled polyester; viscose is made from wood pulp, so this confuses recycling with regeneration."]}
{"id":"gdt-093","s":"dt","t":"3.2.5","lv":1,"q":"What is one advantage of kiln seasoning over air seasoning of timber?","o":["It uses no energy, so it costs less","It leaves extra moisture in the timber so it bends more easily","It is much faster and the moisture content can be controlled to a low level","It can be done while the tree is still growing"],"a":2,"x":"A kiln dries timber in days or weeks and the moisture level can be controlled. Air seasoning takes months or years. The trap is thinking kilns are cheaper, but they use energy.","w":["Kilns need energy for heat and fans, so air seasoning is the low-energy, cheaper method.","Seasoning removes moisture, and timber left wet would warp and split rather than bend usefully.","","Seasoning is done to timber after the tree is felled and cut, not to a growing tree."]}
{"id":"gdt-094","s":"dt","t":"3.2.5","lv":2,"q":"A high carbon steel chisel has been hardened and is now too brittle. Which process should be done next, and why?","o":["Tempering: reheat gently to a set temperature, then cool, to reduce brittleness","Annealing: heat to red and cool slowly, to make it as hard as possible","Hardening again: heat to red and quench, to make it tougher","Work hardening: hammer it cold, to make it softer"],"a":0,"x":"Hardened high carbon steel is hard but brittle. Tempering reheats it to a lower temperature to reduce brittleness while keeping most of the hardness. The trap is thinking annealing makes steel harder.","w":["","Annealing makes metal soft and ductile, not hard, so this confuses annealing with hardening.","Quenching again leaves the steel just as hard and brittle, so it does not fix brittleness.","Work hardening makes metal harder and more brittle, not softer, so it would make things worse."]}
{"id":"gdt-095","s":"dt","t":"3.2.5","lv":2,"q":"Plywood is less likely to split along the grain than the same thickness of solid timber. What is the main reason?","o":["The glue between layers is stronger than the wood","It is always made from hardwood, which never splits","It has been seasoned for much longer than solid timber","Each layer has its grain at right angles to the next one"],"a":3,"x":"Plywood is laminated from veneers. Each layer's grain runs at 90° to the layer next to it. This spreads the strength in both directions and stops splitting along one grain direction.","w":["The glue holds the layers together, but the resistance to splitting comes from the crossed grain.","Plywood is often made from softwood, and hardwoods can split too, so this is false.","Seasoning time does not stop splitting; the alternating grain direction of the layers does.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-096","s":"dt","t":"3.2.5","lv":2,"q":"Acrylic sheet can be heated and reshaped many times. A melamine formaldehyde worktop cannot. Why?","o":["Melamine formaldehyde has no polymer chains, so it cannot soften","Acrylic is thermoforming; melamine formaldehyde is thermosetting and cross-linked","Acrylic has strong cross-links between its chains, so they slide when heated","Melamine formaldehyde loses its cross-links when first heated, so it breaks down"],"a":1,"x":"Thermoforming polymers like acrylic have chains without strong cross-links, so heat softens them and they can be reshaped. Thermosetting polymers have cross-links that stay fixed. The trap is swapping the two types.","w":["Melamine formaldehyde is a polymer with chains, and cross-links between them stop it softening.","","This is reversed, as cross-links make thermosetting polymers rigid while acrylic has none.","Cross-links form when a thermoset is first cured and stay in place, so heating cannot remove them."]}
{"id":"gdt-097","s":"dt","t":"3.2.5","lv":3,"q":"A student hammers copper sheet into a bowl. It becomes hard and springy and starts to crack. What should they do to carry on shaping it?","o":["Hammer faster while cold so the hardness spreads out evenly","Season it for a few weeks so the stress can leave the metal","Anneal it: heat to red heat, let it cool, then carry on shaping","Case harden it so the surface becomes tougher"],"a":2,"x":"Hammering and bending cold copper causes work hardening. Annealing (heating to red heat then cooling) makes it soft and ductile again so shaping can continue. The trap is using a process that adds hardness.","w":["More cold hammering adds to the work hardening, making cracking even more likely.","Seasoning is a timber process, so this confuses it with annealing metal.","","Case hardening is for low carbon steel and adds hardness, so it would not make copper workable."]}
{"id":"gdt-098","s":"dt","t":"3.2.6","lv":1,"q":"A maker is building a light frame from steel. Which stock form of steel is hollow, so it is lighter than a solid bar of the same outside width?","o":["Round bar","Tube","Angle","Flat bar"],"a":1,"x":"Tube is hollow along its length, so it uses less metal and is lighter. Round bar and flat bar are solid. Angle has an open L-shaped section, so it is not a hollow section.","w":["Round bar is solid all the way through, so being round does not make it hollow or lighter.","","Angle is an open L-shaped section, not a closed hollow section like a tube.","Flat bar is a solid rectangular strip, and being thin does not make it hollow."]}
{"id":"gdt-099","s":"dt","t":"3.2.6","lv":1,"q":"A sheet of A3 paper is cut in half, across its longer side, to make two equal sheets. What standard paper size is each sheet?","o":["A2","A5","A6","A4"],"a":3,"x":"In the A series, each time a sheet is halved the number goes up by one. So A3 halved gives two A4 sheets. The trap is thinking a smaller sheet has a smaller number.","w":["A2 is double the size of A3, because a smaller number means a bigger sheet.","A5 is half of A4, so it needs two halvings of A3, not one.","A6 needs three halvings of A3, so this counts too many cuts.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-100","s":"dt","t":"3.2.6","lv":2,"q":"Fabric is 150 cm wide and costs £6 per metre, sold by the cm. Four rectangular pieces, each 60 cm wide and 80 cm long, are needed. All are cut the same way round. What is the lowest cost?","o":["£9.60","£19.20","£7.68","£4.80"],"a":0,"x":"Two 60 cm pieces fit across 150 cm. Four pieces need two rows of 80 cm, which is 1.6 m. 1.6 × £6 = £9.60. Using area alone gives £7.68, but pieces must fit the fabric width.","w":["","Laid the four pieces in one line (4 × 0.8 m = 3.2 m), ignoring that two fit across the width.","Divided total area by the fabric width (1.28 m), ignoring that whole pieces must fit the width.","Bought only 0.8 m, as if all four pieces fit across, but 4 × 60 cm is more than 150 cm."]}
{"id":"gdt-101","s":"dt","t":"3.2.6","lv":2,"q":"Aluminium bar comes in stock lengths of 2000 mm. A maker needs 5 pieces, each 700 mm long. Ignore the width of the saw cuts. What is the smallest number of stock bars needed?","o":["2","4","3","5"],"a":2,"x":"Each 2000 mm bar gives only 2 pieces of 700 mm (a third needs 2100 mm). So 5 pieces need 3 bars. Dividing total length (3500 ÷ 2000 = 1.75) wrongly assumes offcuts can be joined.","w":["Rounded up 3500 ÷ 2000 = 1.75, but the 600 mm offcuts are too short for a 700 mm piece.","Added a spare bar for waste, but three bars already give six pieces, which is enough.","","Used one bar per piece, but each bar can give two 700 mm pieces."]}
{"id":"gdt-102","s":"dt","t":"3.2.6","lv":3,"q":"A 1000 mm × 500 mm acrylic sheet is used to cut 8 rectangles, each 200 mm × 150 mm. The rest of the sheet is scrap. What percentage of the sheet is wasted?","o":["48%","52%","108%","26%"],"a":1,"x":"Sheet area is 500 000 mm². Eight pieces use 8 × 30 000 = 240 000 mm². Waste is 260 000 mm². 260 000 ÷ 500 000 = 52%. The traps are giving the percentage used (48%) or dividing by the wrong area.","w":["240 000 ÷ 500 000 = 48% is the percentage used, not the percentage wasted.","","Divided the waste by the used area (260 000 ÷ 240 000) instead of by the whole sheet area.","Divided the waste area by 1 000 000 instead of the sheet area of 500 000 mm²."]}
{"id":"gdt-103","s":"dt","t":"3.2.7","lv":1,"q":"Which product is most suited to continuous production?","o":["A bespoke engagement ring made for one customer","A run of 50 posters for a school play","A test model of a new kettle","Sheet steel made 24 hours a day, every day"],"a":3,"x":"Continuous production runs non-stop, often 24 hours a day, to make very large amounts of one product. Sheet steel is a good fit. The ring is one-off, the posters a batch, and the kettle test model is a prototype.","w":["A bespoke ring is a one-off product, made once to suit one customer.","A small run of 50 is batch production, not non-stop production.","A test model is a prototype; only one is made so the design can be tested.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-104","s":"dt","t":"3.2.7","lv":1,"q":"What is the main purpose of making a prototype?","o":["To test and improve a design before it is made in larger numbers","To make as many products as possible at the lowest cost","To keep a factory running day and night","To sell to customers as the final version of the product"],"a":0,"x":"A prototype is a first working model, made to test and evaluate a design. Problems can then be fixed before costly tooling or large-scale production. Making many cheaply describes mass production, not prototyping.","w":["","Making large numbers cheaply is the aim of mass production, not a prototype.","Running non-stop is continuous production; a prototype is usually just one model.","A prototype is for testing and is not normally the finished version for sale."]}
{"id":"gdt-105","s":"dt","t":"3.2.7","lv":2,"q":"A firm moves from making 10 of a product to making 100,000. Why does the cost of each product usually fall?","o":["Workers are always paid less for each hour they work","The design is made simpler so it needs less material","Machines in a factory use no energy when making many products","Set-up costs, such as tooling, are shared between many more products"],"a":3,"x":"Tooling, jigs and machine set-up cost the same however many products are made. Spreading that cost over 100,000 products makes each one cheaper. Pay rates and energy use do not simply vanish in mass production.","w":["Pay rates do not drop because output is higher; the saving is in shared set-up costs.","Nothing says the design changes; the saving comes from spreading fixed costs.","Machines still use energy; the saving is in sharing tooling costs across more products.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-106","s":"dt","t":"3.2.7","lv":2,"q":"A company sells scarves in a new colour each season. It makes about 2,000 of each colour. What is the main advantage of using batch production?","o":["The lowest possible cost for each scarf at any quantity","The machines can be reset to make a different colour for each batch","Tooling is never needed, so there are no set-up costs","Production never stops, so there is no downtime"],"a":1,"x":"Batch production makes a set number, then the machines are reset for the next product. This flexibility suits changing designs or colours. It does not give the lowest unit cost, which mass production does, and it has set-up costs and stops.","w":["Mass production gives the lowest unit cost; batch costs more per item.","","Batch production still needs set-up costs, such as changing machines and moulds.","Batch production stops between batches for changeovers; continuous production is the non-stop one."]}
{"id":"gdt-107","s":"dt","t":"3.2.7","lv":3,"q":"Each batch has a set-up cost of £600. Making each item costs £4 in materials and labour. How much less does each item cost in a batch of 600 than in a batch of 300?","o":["£2","£5","£6","£1"],"a":3,"x":"Batch of 300: £600 ÷ 300 + £4 = £6 each. Batch of 600: £600 ÷ 600 + £4 = £5 each. The saving is £1. The trap is to stop after finding one set-up share, or to give one of the unit costs.","w":["£2 is the set-up share for 300 items, not the saving; the £4 item cost is not included.","£5 is the cost of each item in the 600 batch, not the difference.","£6 is the cost of each item in the 300 batch, not the difference.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-108","s":"dt","t":"3.2.8","lv":1,"q":"A company makes 500 identical acrylic key rings. What is the main reason to use a CNC laser cutter instead of cutting each one by hand?","o":["It does not need a drawing or file to follow","It can cut any material at any thickness","It repeats the same accurate cut every time","It removes the need to check the finished parts"],"a":2,"x":"A CNC machine follows a program, so each part is cut the same. This gives repeatable accuracy in batch production. The trap is thinking it removes all checking. Quality checks are still needed.","w":["A CNC machine needs a CAD file or program to follow, so this is wrong.","Machines have limits on material and thickness, so this overstates what a laser cutter can do.","","Finished parts still need quality checks, as tools wear and settings can be wrong."]}
{"id":"gdt-109","s":"dt","t":"3.2.8","lv":1,"q":"A factory inspects a sample of parts every hour. It rejects any part outside the tolerance. What is this checking called?","o":["Quality control","Quality assurance","Just-in-time delivery","Prototyping"],"a":0,"x":"Quality control means inspecting and testing products to see if they meet the standard. Quality assurance is different. It is the planned system of procedures that aims to prevent faults happening.","w":["","Quality assurance is the system that prevents faults, not the checking of the parts themselves.","Just-in-time is a way of getting stock when needed. It is not about checking parts.","Prototyping means making a model to test a design. It is not a check on production parts."]}
{"id":"gdt-110","s":"dt","t":"3.2.8","lv":2,"q":"A drawing shows a hole as 8.00 mm, +0.10 mm / −0.05 mm. What is the smallest diameter that is still accepted?","o":["7.90 mm","8.05 mm","8.10 mm","7.95 mm"],"a":3,"x":"The lower limit is 8.00 − 0.05 = 7.95 mm. The trap is using the +0.10 figure on both sides. This gives 7.90 mm, but the tolerance is not equal both ways.","w":["This takes 0.10 off instead of 0.05, wrongly using the upper tolerance for the lower limit.","This adds 0.05 to 8.00. That is not a limit, since the upper limit uses +0.10.","This is the largest diameter allowed, not the smallest.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-111","s":"dt","t":"3.2.8","lv":2,"q":"A workshop must cut 200 wooden rails to 150 mm ±1 mm on a saw. Which method is quickest and keeps every rail within tolerance?","o":["Mark each rail with a tape measure and pencil, then cut by eye","Fix a stop block 150 mm from the blade and push each rail against it","Cut each rail slightly long, then trim it by eye to look right","Mark the next rail using the last rail that was cut"],"a":1,"x":"A stop block sets the length once, so every rail is the same. Copying from the last cut rail is a trap. Small errors add up with each copy, and rails drift outside the ±1 mm tolerance.","w":["Marking each rail separately is slow, and reading and cutting by eye gives small errors.","","Trimming by eye is slow and not accurate enough to hold a ±1 mm tolerance.","Copying from the last cut piece makes errors build up, so later rails drift out of tolerance."]}
{"id":"gdt-112","s":"dt","t":"3.2.8","lv":3,"q":"A part must be 75.00 mm ±0.25 mm. Five parts measure 74.70, 74.75, 75.10, 75.25 and 75.30 mm. How many parts are within tolerance?","o":["1","2","3","4"],"a":2,"x":"The accepted range is 74.75 mm to 75.25 mm, and the limits count as passing. So 74.75, 75.10 and 75.25 pass. 74.70 and 75.30 fail. The trap is leaving out a part exactly on a limit.","w":["Only 75.10 is inside the limits, but parts exactly on a limit still pass.","This leaves out one of the parts exactly on a limit, but they are accepted.","","This accepts one of the parts that is outside the range, 74.70 or 75.30, which fails."]}
{"id":"gdt-113","s":"dt","t":"3.2.9","lv":1,"q":"Which surface treatment thickens the natural oxide layer on aluminium, making it harder wearing and able to take dye?","o":["Galvanising","Anodising","Electroplating","Tanalising"],"a":1,"x":"Anodising uses an electric current to build up the oxide layer on aluminium. Electroplating adds a thin coat of a different metal instead. Galvanising is for steel and tanalising is for timber.","w":["Galvanising coats steel with zinc; it does not thicken aluminium's oxide layer.","","Electroplating adds a layer of another metal, which confuses coating with thickening the oxide layer.","Tanalising is a preservative treatment for timber, so it is not used on metal."]}
{"id":"gdt-114","s":"dt","t":"3.2.9","lv":1,"q":"A café's card menus keep getting spilled drinks on them. Which finish makes the card waterproof and wipe-clean?","o":["Embossing","Foil blocking","Die cutting","Laminating"],"a":3,"x":"Laminating seals the card in a thin plastic film, so spills wipe off. Embossing and foil blocking only change the look. Die cutting is a shaping process, not a finish. The trap is picking a decorative finish.","w":["Embossing makes a decorative raised pattern and does not make card waterproof.","Foil blocking adds a shiny metallic design for looks, not a waterproof layer.","Die cutting cuts the card to shape, so it is a forming process, not a surface finish.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-115","s":"dt","t":"3.2.9","lv":2,"q":"A steel bike frame is sprayed with electrostatically charged plastic powder. What must happen next to finish the powder coating?","o":["It is baked in an oven so the powder melts into a smooth coat","It is left to air dry until the solvent has evaporated","It is dipped in molten zinc to seal the powder in place","It is rubbed with wax and buffed to a shine"],"a":0,"x":"Powder coating needs heat. The frame is baked so the powder melts and fuses into a tough, even layer. The trap is treating it like paint. The powder has no solvent, so air drying does not work.","w":["","Powder contains no solvent, so air drying will not fuse it; this treats it like wet paint.","Dipping in molten zinc is galvanising, a different process that would not seal plastic powder.","Waxing and buffing is a polishing step that cannot melt the powder into a coat."]}
{"id":"gdt-117","s":"dt","t":"3.2.9","lv":3,"q":"A galvanised steel gate is scratched, exposing bare steel. The steel next to the scratch still does not rust quickly. Why?","o":["Zinc melts slightly and flows back to fill the scratch","Zinc reacts first, so it is used up instead of the steel","Steel cannot rust once it has been touched by zinc","Zinc only acts as a barrier, so the steel rusts as normal"],"a":1,"x":"Zinc is more reactive than steel, so it corrodes first and protects nearby exposed steel. This is sacrificial protection. A painted coat only works as a barrier, so rust starts once it is scratched.","w":["Zinc does not melt or flow at outdoor temperatures, so this invents a self-healing effect.","","Zinc only protects nearby steel while the zinc lasts, so steel can still rust later.","This is true of paint, but zinc protects the steel even after the coat is broken."]}
{"id":"gdt-118","s":"dt","t":"3.3.1","lv":1,"q":"A designer is told to create a new lunch box for primary school children. What is a design brief?","o":["A list of measurable requirements the product must meet","A step-by-step plan for making the product in a factory","A short statement saying what is to be designed and who it is for","A table showing the cost of materials and labour"],"a":2,"x":"A design brief is a short statement of the problem: what is to be designed and for whom. Measurable requirements belong in the design specification. Do not mix up the two.","w":["This describes a design specification, not the brief, which is shorter and less detailed.","This describes a production plan or manufacturing specification, not a brief stating the problem.","","A costing table covers only money, not the problem to be solved or the user."]}
{"id":"gdt-119","s":"dt","t":"3.3.1","lv":1,"q":"A designer is making a child's scooter. Which of these is secondary data?","o":["Measuring the hand sizes of ten children at a school","Watching children ride scooters in a park","Interviewing parents about what they want","Reading a published table of average hand sizes by age"],"a":3,"x":"Secondary data was collected by someone else and is already published, like an anthropometric table. Measuring, observing and interviewing are done first-hand, so they give primary data.","w":["The designer takes these measurements personally, so this is primary data, not secondary.","Observation is done first-hand, so it gives primary data, not secondary data.","Interviews are carried out by the designer, so this is primary data.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-120","s":"dt","t":"3.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A designer is writing a design specification for a garden trowel. Which point is best, because it can be measured and tested?","o":["The handle must be 100–120 mm long so most adults can grip it","The handle should look nice and feel good to hold","The trowel should be a modern design that people will want","The trowel must be made by someone who likes gardening"],"a":0,"x":"Good specification points are clear and measurable, such as a size range linked to the user. Vague words like 'nice' or 'modern' cannot be tested. The trap is writing opinions instead of criteria.","w":["","'Nice' and 'feel good' are vague opinions that cannot be measured or tested.","'Modern' and 'want' are vague and cannot be checked against a clear target.","This is about the maker, not the product, so it sets no testable requirement."]}
{"id":"gdt-121","s":"dt","t":"3.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Which statement belongs in a manufacturing specification, not in a design specification, for a batch of 200 acrylic key fobs?","o":["The key fob must be safe for children aged 8 and over","The key fob must be small enough to fit in a trouser pocket","The key fob parts must be cut to a tolerance of ±0.5 mm","The key fob must appeal to teenagers"],"a":2,"x":"A manufacturing specification gives details for making the product: materials, processes, quantity and tolerances. The other statements describe what the product must do or who it is for, which is design specification content.","w":["This is a safety requirement for the product, so it belongs in the design specification.","This is a size requirement linked to the user, so it belongs in the design specification.","","This describes the target market and appeal, so it belongs in the design specification."]}
{"id":"gdt-122","s":"dt","t":"3.3.1","lv":3,"q":"A designer finds a 1998 survey on teenagers' phone habits and wants to use it to design a phone case today. What is the best next step?","o":["Use it unchanged, as published data is always reliable","Ignore it, as secondary data must never be used","Check it against new primary data from today's teenagers","Treat it as primary data, as the designer found it themselves"],"a":2,"x":"Secondary data can be old or collected for a different group. Checking it with up-to-date primary data from the target users makes the research more reliable. Do not assume published means correct.","w":["Published data can be out of date; phone habits have changed hugely since 1998.","Secondary data is useful and allowed; it just needs checking for age and relevance.","","Finding data is not collecting it; this survey was run by others, so it is secondary."]}
{"id":"gdt-123","s":"dt","t":"3.3.2","lv":1,"q":"Why does deforestation add to rising carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere?","o":["Living trees give out more CO₂ than they take in","Fewer trees absorb CO₂, and burnt or rotting wood releases it","Sawmills use up all the oxygen in the air","Trees make CO₂ from sunlight and water"],"a":1,"x":"Trees take in CO₂ as they grow. When forests are cleared, less CO₂ is absorbed. Burning or rotting wood also releases stored carbon. The trap is thinking trees are a source of CO₂.","w":["Wrong: growing trees take in more CO₂ than they release. This reverses what trees do.","","Wrong: sawmills do not use up the air's oxygen. This is a made-up effect, not the real carbon cycle.","Wrong: trees take in CO₂ for photosynthesis. They do not make it from sunlight and water."]}
{"id":"gdt-124","s":"dt","t":"3.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A designer is making a garden bench from wood. She wants to avoid adding to deforestation. Which choice is best?","o":["Any hardwood, because wood is a natural material","Cheap imported hardwood with no certificate, to keep costs down","FSC-certified timber from a managed forest where trees are replanted","Timber from cleared rainforest, because it is cheaper to ship"],"a":2,"x":"FSC certification shows the timber comes from a well-managed forest where trees are replanted. The trap is believing all wood is automatically sustainable because it is natural.","w":["Natural does not mean sustainably sourced. Some hardwood comes from forests that are cut down faster than they regrow.","With no certificate, the timber could come from illegal or unmanaged logging. Low cost is not the aim here.","","Timber from cleared rainforest directly adds to deforestation. Shipping cost is not the issue."]}
{"id":"gdt-125","s":"dt","t":"3.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A designer wants to use Fairtrade cotton for a tote bag. Which is a likely limitation of this choice?","o":["The raw material may cost more, so the bag's price may rise","Fairtrade cotton cannot be dyed or printed on","Fairtrade cotton is a synthetic fibre that will not biodegrade","Fairtrade rules do not allow cotton to be used for bags"],"a":0,"x":"Fairtrade pays producers a fair price, so the material can cost more. This may raise the price or cut the profit. The trap is thinking Fairtrade changes how the material behaves. It only changes how it is sourced.","w":["","Wrong: Fairtrade is about how the cotton is sourced, not its properties. It can be dyed and printed like other cotton.","Wrong: cotton is a natural fibre, and Fairtrade does not change that.","Wrong: Fairtrade has no rule about which products cotton can be used for."]}
{"id":"gdt-126","s":"dt","t":"3.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A company sells wooden toys made abroad and flown to the UK by air. Which change would best reduce the carbon footprint of each toy?","o":["Paint the toys in brighter colours","Wrap each toy in extra layers of packaging","Make each toy from more separate parts","Ship the toys by sea instead of by air"],"a":3,"x":"Air freight gives out far more CO₂ per tonne carried than sea freight, so switching to sea cuts the footprint. The trap is choosing a change that does not affect energy use, or one that adds weight and materials.","w":["Paint colour does not change transport emissions. It is not linked to the carbon footprint problem.","Extra packaging adds materials and weight, so it would raise the footprint, not cut it.","More parts mean more processes and more materials, which would raise the footprint.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-127","s":"dt","t":"3.3.2","lv":3,"q":"A designer is thinking about using recycled aluminium instead of new aluminium from mined ore. Which pair correctly gives an opportunity and a limitation?","o":["It is a renewable resource, but it costs more to mine","It uses less energy than new metal, but mixed scrap may need sorting","It releases no CO₂ at all, but it can only be recycled once","It needs no melting, but it is weaker than new metal"],"a":1,"x":"Recycling aluminium needs much less energy than extracting it from ore, which cuts CO₂ and mining damage. But scrap can be mixed alloys, so it needs sorting. Aluminium is finite, not renewable.","w":["Aluminium is a finite resource, not renewable. Recycled metal also does not need to be mined.","","Recycling still uses energy and gives off some CO₂. Aluminium can also be recycled many times.","Recycled aluminium must still be melted. Its properties can match new aluminium when properly processed."]}
{"id":"gdt-128","s":"dt","t":"3.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Harry Beck's 1931 map of the London Underground was a new idea. What did it do differently from earlier maps?","o":["It showed every street and building at true scale","It used straight lines and equal gaps, not true distances","It used 3D drawings of each station","It listed the fare for each journey"],"a":1,"x":"Beck made a diagram, not a geographic map. Lines run straight, horizontally, vertically or at 45°, so routes and interchanges are easy to read. The trap is thinking a good map must be to scale.","w":["Beck dropped true scale and street detail, so this describes the opposite of his idea.","","Beck's map was a flat diagram. It did not use 3D drawings of stations.","Fares were not the point of Beck's design. It focused on routes and connections."]}
{"id":"gdt-129","s":"dt","t":"3.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Alessi is an Italian company known for kitchenware and everyday objects. How does Alessi usually develop its products?","o":["It copies popular products from other companies","It only uses designers who work full-time in its factory","It makes only cheap products for the mass market","It asks well-known outside designers to create products"],"a":3,"x":"Alessi works with many respected outside designers, such as Philippe Starck and Michael Graves. The trap is assuming a company only uses its own in-house staff.","w":["Alessi is known for original, designer-led products, not copies.","Alessi works with many external designers, not only in-house staff.","Alessi products are often stylish design objects at higher prices, not just cheap goods.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-130","s":"dt","t":"3.3.3","lv":2,"q":"Which set of features best matches Apple's design approach under designer Jonathan Ive?","o":["Simple clean forms, few controls and a focus on the user experience","Bright patterns and decoration so the product stands out","Exposed fixings and wiring shown as a feature","Many buttons, with one for every function"],"a":0,"x":"Apple products under Ive are known for minimal, clean forms, few buttons and easy use. The trap is thinking a more complex look means a better product.","w":["","Apple's style is plain and minimal, not decorated with bright patterns.","Apple hides fixings and wiring to keep a clean, smooth finish.","Apple reduces the number of buttons to keep use simple."]}
{"id":"gdt-131","s":"dt","t":"3.3.3","lv":2,"q":"Marcel Breuer designed the Wassily chair. Which pair of facts about it is correct?","o":["Moulded plywood; Bauhaus","Tubular steel; Arts and Crafts","Tubular steel; Bauhaus","Cast aluminium; Memphis"],"a":2,"x":"Breuer used bent tubular steel for the Wassily chair, an idea linked to bicycle handlebars. He worked at the Bauhaus school. The trap is mixing up designers, materials and movements.","w":["Breuer's chair used bent tubular steel, not moulded plywood.","Arts and Crafts was William Morris's movement. Breuer was linked to the Bauhaus.","","The chair used tubular steel, and Memphis was a later 1980s group unrelated to Breuer."]}
{"id":"gdt-132","s":"dt","t":"3.3.3","lv":3,"q":"A designer redesigns a radio using Dieter Rams' idea that good design is 'as little design as possible'. Which change fits this idea best?","o":["Add patterned panels so it stands out in shops","Remove decoration and keep only the controls users need","Add extra functions so it beats rival radios","Change the style every year to follow fashion"],"a":1,"x":"Rams' 'less, but better' approach removes anything not needed, so the product is clear and long-lasting. The trap is thinking more features or decoration means better design.","w":["Added pattern is decoration. Rams believed unnecessary decoration should be removed.","","Extra functions add clutter. Rams wanted fewer, better features.","Rams wanted long-lasting designs, not ones that follow short-term fashion."]}
{"id":"gdt-133","s":"dt","t":"3.3.4","lv":1,"q":"What does iterative design involve?","o":["Making one final design and never changing it after that","Repeating a cycle of modelling, testing, evaluating and improving a design","Asking one user for their opinion at the very start only","Copying a successful existing product exactly as it is"],"a":1,"x":"Iterative design means going round a cycle again and again: model, test, evaluate, improve. Each pass makes the design better. The trap is thinking a design is finished after one attempt.","w":["Iterative design expects many changes, so keeping one unchanged design is the opposite of iteration.","","Iteration repeats testing throughout the project, not just one opinion gathered at the start.","Copying is not iteration, which improves your own design using test results."]}
{"id":"gdt-134","s":"dt","t":"3.3.4","lv":1,"q":"A designer is using a user-centred design approach. Which action fits this approach best?","o":["Choosing the cheapest materials before doing any user research","Using the design that she personally likes the most","Making the product look as unusual as she possibly can","Asking users to try a prototype and using their feedback to improve it"],"a":3,"x":"User-centred design puts the needs and wants of the people who will use the product first. Users test prototypes and their feedback guides changes. The trap is designing for yourself.","w":["Starting with cost before any research ignores user needs, so it is not user-centred.","Designing for your own taste ignores what the user actually needs and wants.","An unusual look alone may not meet user needs, so it is not user-centred.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-135","s":"dt","t":"3.3.4","lv":2,"q":"A school team designing a new bag has a designer, a manufacturer and a student user working together. What is the main benefit of this collaboration?","o":["Different skills and views lead to a wider range of ideas and fewer problems","The design is finished faster because only one person decides","It guarantees the product will be the cheapest on sale","It removes the need to test a prototype"],"a":0,"x":"Collaboration means working with others. Different skills and viewpoints give more ideas and spot problems early. The trap is thinking more people always means less testing or lower cost.","w":["","Collaboration means several people share decisions, so it is not one person deciding.","Collaboration does not guarantee a low price, because cost depends on materials and manufacture.","Prototypes still need testing however many people help, so this is a false shortcut."]}
{"id":"gdt-136","s":"dt","t":"3.3.4","lv":2,"q":"Which action is most likely to help a student avoid design fixation when designing a desk lamp?","o":["Developing their very first idea in great detail straight away","Copying the best-selling lamp they can find in the shop","Sketching many different ideas, using sources such as nature, before choosing","Asking only one friend for an idea and then using that idea"],"a":2,"x":"Design fixation is sticking to one idea. Sketching many varied ideas, from different sources of inspiration, widens thinking. The trap is committing early to a first idea or copying one existing product.","w":["Developing the first idea early is how fixation starts, because it stops other ideas appearing.","Copying one product limits thinking to that single solution, so it increases fixation.","","Relying on one person's idea narrows the range of ideas, so it does not avoid fixation."]}
{"id":"gdt-137","s":"dt","t":"3.3.4","lv":3,"q":"A student makes a prototype lunch box. Children test it and find the lid too hard to open. She changes the lid, makes a new prototype and tests it again. Which strategies is she using?","o":["Design fixation and collaboration","User-centred design and iterative design","Iterative design and design fixation","User-centred design and design fixation"],"a":1,"x":"She uses users' feedback to meet their needs, which is user-centred design. She changes, remakes and retests, which is iterative design. Design fixation would mean refusing to change her first idea.","w":["She changed her design after testing, so she is not showing design fixation.","","Changing the lid shows she is not fixated, because fixation means sticking with one idea.","Using feedback is user-centred, but changing the lid shows she is not fixated."]}
{"id":"gdt-138","s":"dt","t":"3.3.5","lv":1,"q":"On a third angle working drawing, how are edges that are hidden from view, such as a hole inside a block, normally shown?","o":["As thick solid lines","As thin dashed lines","As long and short chain lines","As thin wavy lines"],"a":1,"x":"Hidden detail is drawn with thin dashed lines. Thick solid lines show visible outlines. Chain lines are a different convention, used for centre lines, so they are a common mix-up.","w":["Thick solid lines show visible outlines, so hidden edges would look as if they could be seen.","","Chain lines show centres of holes or lines of symmetry, not hidden edges. This mixes up two line types.","Wavy lines are freehand break lines, not the standard line for hidden detail."]}
{"id":"gdt-139","s":"dt","t":"3.3.5","lv":1,"q":"A designer wants to show how the parts of a flat-pack bookcase fit together during assembly. Which type of drawing is most suitable?","o":["A flow chart","A plan view only","A perspective drawing of the finished bookcase","An exploded view"],"a":3,"x":"An exploded view pulls the parts apart along lines to show how they fit and in what order. A drawing of the finished product hides the joints and fixings, which is the usual trap.","w":["A flow chart shows the steps in a process, not how the parts of an object fit together.","A plan view shows only the top, so it cannot show how every part fits.","A drawing of the finished bookcase hides the joints and fixings, so assembly cannot be seen.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-140","s":"dt","t":"3.3.5","lv":2,"q":"A drawing of a table leg is made at a scale of 1:5. The real leg is 350 mm long. How long should the leg be on the drawing?","o":["70 mm","1750 mm","345 mm","7 mm"],"a":0,"x":"A scale of 1:5 means the drawing is five times smaller than the real object. Divide the real length by 5: 350 ÷ 5 = 70 mm. Multiplying by 5 is the common slip.","w":["","Multiplying by 5 makes the drawing bigger than the real leg. A 1:5 scale needs dividing.","Subtracting 5 treats the scale as a difference rather than a ratio.","Dividing by 50 shows a slip with the ratio. The real length should be divided by 5, not 50."]}
{"id":"gdt-141","s":"dt","t":"3.3.5","lv":2,"q":"A sketch of a box shows the front face as its true shape, a perfect rectangle. The depth is drawn with parallel lines going back at 45°. What type of drawing is this?","o":["Isometric","Two-point perspective","Oblique","Third angle orthographic"],"a":2,"x":"In oblique drawing the front face is drawn as its true shape and the depth lines are drawn at an angle, often 45°. Isometric drawings use 30° lines and show no face as a true shape.","w":["Isometric uses 30° lines and no face is drawn as a true rectangle. This is a common mix-up.","Perspective drawings use vanishing points and lines that get closer together. These lines are parallel.","","Orthographic drawings show separate flat views, not one 3D view with depth lines."]}
{"id":"gdt-142","s":"dt","t":"3.3.5","lv":3,"q":"A block has a round hole drilled all the way through from front to back. How does the hole appear in the plan view of a third angle drawing?","o":["As a full circle drawn with a solid line","As a dashed circle","As two parallel dashed lines running front to back","It does not appear in this view"],"a":2,"x":"The hole is round only when seen from the front. From above it is hidden, so it shows as two parallel dashed hidden lines the same length as the block's depth. Drawing the circle is the common trap.","w":["A circle is how the hole looks in the front view. The plan view looks from above, where the hole is hidden.","The hole only looks circular from the front, where it is visible and drawn solid; from above it is not a circle.","","The hole is hidden, not absent; hidden detail is shown with dashed lines so the hole is clear."]}
{"id":"gdt-143","s":"dt","t":"3.3.6","lv":1,"q":"A designer makes a quick card model of a new torch handle before using plastic. What is the main advantage of using card at this stage?","o":["It is as strong as the final plastic","It is cheap and quick to change","It shows the exact final finish","It proves the torch is waterproof"],"a":1,"x":"Card is cheap and easy to cut and alter, so size and shape can be tested quickly. The trap is thinking a model has the same strength or finish as the final material.","w":["Card is weaker than plastic, so it cannot show the final strength.","","A card model cannot copy the final surface finish of plastic.","Card is not waterproof, so a card model cannot test waterproofing."]}
{"id":"gdt-144","s":"dt","t":"3.3.6","lv":1,"q":"What does 'iterative design' mean when developing a prototype?","o":["Making one design and selling it straight away","Making many identical copies of one prototype","Repeating a cycle of designing, making, testing and improving","Copying a rival's product and changing the colour"],"a":2,"x":"Iterative design is a repeating cycle: model, test, get feedback, then improve. The trap is thinking the first prototype is the final one.","w":["Iterative design includes testing and improving first; it is not selling the first attempt.","Making identical copies is production, not improving a design through repeated testing.","","Copying a rival's product is not a design cycle based on testing and feedback."]}
{"id":"gdt-145","s":"dt","t":"3.3.6","lv":2,"q":"A prototype phone stand must hold a phone at 60° and support 300 g. Which test best checks it against this specification?","o":["Put a 300 g mass on it and measure the angle with a protractor","Ask a friend whether they like the colour and shape","Weigh the empty stand on a set of kitchen scales","Count how many stands can be made in one hour"],"a":0,"x":"Tests should match the specification points. A 300 g load checks strength and a protractor checks the angle. The trap is choosing opinions or measures that do not test the stated requirements.","w":["","An opinion on looks does not check the load or angle in the specification.","The stand's own mass does not show if it supports 300 g or holds 60°.","Output rate relates to manufacturing, not whether the prototype meets the specification."]}
{"id":"gdt-146","s":"dt","t":"3.3.6","lv":2,"q":"A prototype costs £4.80 per item to make in a batch. It is sold to a shop for £9.50. What is the profit for a batch of 200 items?","o":["£960","£1,060","£1,900","£940"],"a":3,"x":"Profit per item is £9.50 − £4.80 = £4.70. For 200 items, £4.70 × 200 = £940. Traps are giving the cost or the income instead of the profit.","w":["£960 is the total cost (£4.80 × 200), not the profit.","This comes from a subtraction slip, using £5.30 profit per item instead of £4.70.","£1,900 is the total income (£9.50 × 200); the cost has not been taken off.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-147","s":"dt","t":"3.3.6","lv":3,"q":"Tests on a bike light prototype show: brightness meets the specification, waterproofing passes, but the battery lasts 3 hours against a 5 hour target. What should the designer do next?","o":["Accept it, as two of three tests passed","Change the battery or LED to use less power, then retest","Change the casing colour and retest brightness","Remove the waterproof seals to save weight"],"a":1,"x":"Every specification point must be met. The failed test shows the problem, so the design is modified to extend battery life and then retested. The trap is accepting a partial pass or changing unrelated features.","w":["A prototype must meet all specification points, so a partial pass is not enough.","","Colour does not affect battery life, so this does not fix the failed test.","Removing seals would risk failing the waterproof test and does not improve battery life."]}
{"id":"gdt-148","s":"dt","t":"3.3.7","lv":1,"q":"A designer is choosing a material for the handle of a kettle. Which property is the most important for the handle?","o":["Good thermal conductivity","Good thermal insulation","High hardness","High ductility"],"a":1,"x":"A kettle handle must stay safe to hold while the kettle is hot. A thermal insulator stops heat passing to the user's hand. A conductor would be a trap, as it would burn them.","w":["A conductor would pass heat to the user's hand; this confuses conductor with insulator.","","Hardness resists scratching and wear but does not stop heat reaching the hand.","Ductility is the ability to be drawn into wire; it has nothing to do with heat safety."]}
{"id":"gdt-149","s":"dt","t":"3.3.7","lv":1,"q":"A designer chooses timber that carries the FSC logo. What does this logo tell the designer about the timber?","o":["It is cheaper than any other timber","It is stronger than timber without the logo","It can only be a hardwood","It comes from a responsibly managed forest"],"a":3,"x":"The FSC logo shows the timber comes from a forest managed responsibly, so trees are replaced. It is an environmental and ethical factor. It says nothing about price, strength or hardwood versus softwood.","w":["The logo is about sourcing, not price; certified timber is often more expensive.","Certification does not guarantee strength; strength depends on the type of timber.","FSC covers both hardwoods and softwoods, so the logo does not mean hardwood only.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-150","s":"dt","t":"3.3.7","lv":2,"q":"A firm makes 10 000 identical plastic phone cases. Why is ordering the plastic granules in bulk usually a good choice?","o":["The supplier usually charges less for each kilogram","The granules become stronger when stored in large amounts","It removes the need for a mould","It makes the cases easier to recycle"],"a":0,"x":"Suppliers usually give a lower price per kilogram for large orders. This cuts the cost of each case. Bulk buying does not change the plastic's properties or remove the need for tooling.","w":["","Storing material in bulk does not change its properties; this is a made-up benefit.","Injection moulding still needs a mould whatever quantity of granules is bought.","Recyclability depends on the type of plastic, not on how much is bought."]}
{"id":"gdt-151","s":"dt","t":"3.3.7","lv":2,"q":"A bench will stay outside all year with little maintenance. Which material is the best choice for its slats?","o":["Untreated pine","MDF","Teak","Interior-grade plywood"],"a":2,"x":"Teak is a hardwood with natural oils that resist rot and moisture, so it needs little upkeep outdoors. Untreated softwood rots, and MDF and interior plywood absorb water and fail.","w":["Untreated softwood rots outside; the slip is thinking all timber lasts outdoors.","MDF swells and breaks down when wet; it is only suitable for dry indoor use.","","Interior-grade plywood uses glue and veneers not made for damp, so it would delaminate outdoors."]}
{"id":"gdt-152","s":"dt","t":"3.3.7","lv":3,"q":"Each product needs 2.4 m of square tube. Steel costs £3.50 per metre and aluminium costs £5.00 per metre. How much more does aluminium cost for 50 products?","o":["£75","£180","£420","£600"],"a":1,"x":"Tube needed is 2.4 × 50 = 120 m. The price difference is £5.00 − £3.50 = £1.50 per metre. So 120 × 1.50 = £180. The traps are forgetting the 2.4 m or giving one total.","w":["This is £1.50 × 50 and forgets that each product uses 2.4 m of tube.","","This is the total cost of the steel, not the extra cost of aluminium.","This is the total cost of the aluminium, not the difference between the two materials."]}
{"id":"gdt-153","s":"dt","t":"3.3.8","lv":1,"q":"A drawing gives a hole size as 10 mm ± 0.1 mm. What does the ± 0.1 mm tell the maker?","o":["The hole must be exactly 10 mm","The hole can be a little bigger or smaller, within 0.1 mm","The hole must be at least 10.1 mm","The hole can be any size up to 10 mm"],"a":1,"x":"A tolerance is the amount a size is allowed to vary. Here the hole can be from 9.9 mm to 10.1 mm. The trap is thinking the stated size must be met exactly.","w":["Thought tolerance means no variation, but real parts are never perfectly exact, so a small allowed range is given.","","Used only the upper limit and ignored the minus, but ± means the size can go above and below 10 mm.","Thought the tolerance only allows smaller sizes, but ± allows variation both above and below the stated size."]}
{"id":"gdt-154","s":"dt","t":"3.3.8","lv":1,"q":"A factory makes thousands of the same plastic part. Why is it important that every part is made within its tolerance?","o":["So the parts use less plastic","So the parts are all the same colour","So the parts are quicker to design","So any part fits with the other parts it must join"],"a":3,"x":"Working within tolerance keeps parts the same size to within set limits. Then parts are interchangeable and fit together. Colour and material use are different quality or cost issues, not tolerance.","w":["Mixed up tolerance with saving material; tolerance controls size accuracy, not the amount of plastic used.","Confused tolerance with finish; tolerance is about size limits, not colour or appearance.","Confused making with designing; tolerance affects accuracy in manufacture and does not speed up design.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-155","s":"dt","t":"3.3.8","lv":2,"q":"A wooden dowel is drawn as 12 mm ± 0.2 mm. Four dowels are measured. Which one is within tolerance?","o":["11.85 mm","11.7 mm","12.25 mm","12.3 mm"],"a":0,"x":"The limits are 12 − 0.2 = 11.8 mm and 12 + 0.2 = 12.2 mm. Only 11.85 mm is inside. The trap is accepting a dowel that is only slightly outside the limits.","w":["","This is 0.1 mm below the lower limit of 11.8 mm, so it is too small and fails.","This is 0.05 mm over the upper limit of 12.2 mm, and being nearly right is still out of tolerance.","This is 0.1 mm over the upper limit of 12.2 mm, so it fails."]}
{"id":"gdt-156","s":"dt","t":"3.3.8","lv":2,"q":"A designer changes a tolerance from ± 0.5 mm to ± 0.05 mm. What is the most likely effect on making the part?","o":["It is cheaper because the part is more accurate","It needs no checking because the limits are so small","It is usually more costly because it needs more accurate work and checks","It is quicker to make because there is less room for error"],"a":2,"x":"A smaller tolerance means a tighter limit. This usually needs better machines, slower work and more inspection, so cost rises. The trap is thinking more accurate means cheaper.","w":["Linked accuracy with low cost, but tighter tolerances need more precise equipment and cause more rejects, so cost rises.","Tight limits need more checking, not less, because more parts can fall outside them.","","Thought less allowed error makes work faster, but meeting tighter limits usually slows production and adds inspection."]}
{"id":"gdt-157","s":"dt","t":"3.3.8","lv":3,"q":"Three spacer blocks, each drawn as 20 mm ± 0.2 mm, are stacked on top of each other. What is the greatest possible height of the stack?","o":["60.2 mm","60.6 mm","60.0 mm","61.2 mm"],"a":1,"x":"Each block can be up to 20.2 mm. Three blocks give 3 × 20.2 = 60.6 mm. The trap is forgetting that tolerances add up (cumulative error) when parts are stacked.","w":["Added the 0.2 mm tolerance only once, but every block can be oversize, so it is added three times.","","Used the drawn size only and ignored the tolerance, but the question asks for the greatest possible height.","Added the whole 0.4 mm tolerance band to each block, but only the upper 0.2 mm counts for the maximum."]}
{"id":"gdt-158","s":"dt","t":"3.3.9","lv":1,"q":"A workshop makes 50 identical plywood coat hooks. Why is a card or MDF template useful for marking out each hook?","o":["It makes each plywood hook stronger","It gives a quick, matching outline every time","It removes the need to cut each hook out","It means no marking tool is needed at all"],"a":1,"x":"A template is a pattern you draw around. It gives repeat shapes that match and saves measuring each one. It only marks the shape, so you still have to cut it out.","w":["A template only guides the marking line; it does not change the strength of the plywood.","","A template only helps with marking out; each hook still has to be cut out.","You still draw around a template with a pencil or scriber."]}
{"id":"gdt-159","s":"dt","t":"3.3.9","lv":1,"q":"A school makes many wooden boxes. Each needs a hole drilled in the same place. What does a drilling jig do?","o":["It measures the depth of each hole once drilled","It makes the drill bit spin at a faster speed","It keeps the wood cool while it is being drilled","It holds the work and guides the drill to one spot"],"a":3,"x":"A jig holds the part and guides the tool, so each part is made the same without marking out every time. Do not confuse it with a template, which only gives a shape to draw around.","w":["This confuses a jig with a depth gauge; a jig holds and guides, it does not measure.","A jig does not change the drill speed; it only controls where the hole goes.","A jig does not cool the wood; it only holds the work and guides the drill.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-160","s":"dt","t":"3.3.9","lv":2,"q":"A 300 mm × 200 mm acrylic sheet is marked out with 70 mm × 45 mm rectangles in a grid, 70 mm side along the 300 mm edge, with no gaps. What percentage of the sheet is waste?","o":["16%","84%","9.6%","19%"],"a":0,"x":"300 ÷ 70 gives 4 across and 200 ÷ 45 gives 4 down, so 16 pieces. Used area is 16 × 3150 = 50 400 mm². Waste is 9600 of 60 000, which is 16%. The trap is giving the used percentage.","w":["","84% is the percentage of the sheet used, not the waste.","9.6% treats 9600 mm² as a percentage instead of dividing by the sheet area.","19% divides the waste by the used area instead of the whole sheet area."]}
{"id":"gdt-161","s":"dt","t":"3.3.9","lv":2,"q":"When marking out a row of holes along a strip, why is it better to measure every hole from one end of the strip than from the previous hole?","o":["It makes the strip slightly longer overall","It means fewer holes need to be drilled","It stops small errors adding up along the strip","It makes each of the holes come out bigger"],"a":2,"x":"Measuring from one fixed reference point stops each small error being passed on to the next hole. Measuring from the last hole lets errors build up, so a fixed reference edge gives more accurate marking out.","w":["The marking out method does not change the length of the strip.","The number of holes comes from the design, not from how they are measured.","","Hole size depends on the drill bit used, not on how holes are measured."]}
{"id":"gdt-162","s":"dt","t":"3.3.9","lv":3,"q":"A designer must cut 8 identical L-shaped parts from one sheet of plywood. Which plan best reduces waste and keeps the leftover useful?","o":["Space the parts evenly apart so each one is easy to cut","Nest them tightly from one edge, leaving one large offcut","Group the parts in the middle, away from the sheet edges","Turn each part to a different angle so no two match"],"a":1,"x":"Nesting L-shapes tightly from a sheet edge saves material, and shared cut lines mean fewer cuts. One large offcut can be used again. The trap is spacing parts out, which leaves many small, useless scraps.","w":["Spacing parts out leaves many small scraps and wastes material, even though cutting feels easier.","","Working in the middle wastes the edges and leaves awkward scraps; the sheet edges should be used.","Random angles stop the shapes fitting together, leaving gaps, so waste goes up."]}
{"id":"gdt-163","s":"dt","t":"3.3.10","lv":1,"q":"Which tool is used to mark a line parallel to the edge of a piece of timber?","o":["Try square","Sliding bevel","Marking gauge","Centre punch"],"a":2,"x":"A marking gauge has an adjustable stock and a pin. It scribes a line at a set distance along the grain, parallel to the edge. A try square marks lines at 90°.","w":["A try square marks lines at 90° to an edge, so this confuses square lines with parallel lines.","A sliding bevel sets and copies angles other than 90°, so it does not scribe lines parallel to an edge.","","A centre punch makes a dent in metal to start a drill hole and cannot scribe a parallel line on timber."]}
{"id":"gdt-164","s":"dt","t":"3.3.10","lv":1,"q":"A student marks the position of a hole on a sheet of steel. Why do they use a centre punch before drilling?","o":["It makes a small dent that stops the drill bit slipping","It cools the drill bit while the hole is cut","It makes the hole bigger so the drill needs less force","It removes sharp edges left on the metal"],"a":0,"x":"A centre punch makes a small dent at the marked point. The drill tip sits in it and does not wander across smooth metal. It does not cool, enlarge or deburr.","w":["","Cooling the bit is done with cutting fluid, so this mixes up two different workshop steps.","The punch only makes a tiny dent and does not enlarge the hole, so this misunderstands its job.","Removing sharp edges is deburring, done after drilling with a file or countersink bit, not a punch."]}
{"id":"gdt-165","s":"dt","t":"3.3.10","lv":2,"q":"A student must make 50 identical acrylic key rings with the same intricate outline. Why is a laser cutter better than a coping saw?","o":["It can cut any thickness of any material without limits","It is safe to use without guards or extraction as it is computer controlled","It costs less than a coping saw, so it suits small batches","It repeats the CAD shape accurately every time with little effort"],"a":3,"x":"A laser cutter follows the CAD file, so every shape is the same and accurate. Hand sawing 50 intricate shapes would be slow and vary. Lasers still need safety measures and have material limits.","w":["Laser cutters have limits on thickness and material type, so this wrongly assumes they can cut anything.","Computer control does not remove danger, as laser cutters still need enclosed covers and fume extraction.","A laser cutter costs far more than a coping saw; its benefit is accuracy and repeatability, not low cost.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-166","s":"dt","t":"3.3.10","lv":2,"q":"A student is about to drill a hole in a small piece of acrylic on a pillar drill. Which action is correct before switching on?","o":["Hold the acrylic in one hand so it can be turned easily","Remove the chuck key and clamp the acrylic firmly","Wear thick gloves to protect the hands from the bit","Leave the chuck key in so it is easy to find later"],"a":1,"x":"Clamp the work so it cannot spin, and remove the chuck key so it cannot fly out. Never hold small work by hand. Gloves can be caught by rotating parts, so they are not worn.","w":["Small work held by hand can be grabbed and spun by the bit, so it must be clamped instead.","","Gloves can be caught by the rotating bit and pull the hand in, so they are not worn when drilling.","A chuck key left in can be thrown out at high speed when the drill starts, so it must be removed."]}
{"id":"gdt-167","s":"dt","t":"3.3.10","lv":3,"q":"A designer needs a one-off plastic prototype with a hollow inside and curved internal channels. Why is a 3D printer more suitable than a CNC milling machine?","o":["It removes material from a solid block, so inside shapes are easy","It always gives a stronger and smoother surface than milling","It builds the part layer by layer, so hollow spaces can be made without cutting","It does not need a CAD file, so the design can start straight away"],"a":2,"x":"A 3D printer is additive. It adds layers, so hollow parts and internal channels form as it builds. Milling is subtractive, and a cutter cannot reach most inside spaces. A 3D printer still needs a CAD file.","w":["This describes subtractive milling, so it mixes up additive and subtractive processes.","Printed parts often show layer lines and can be weaker, so the advantage is shape freedom, not strength or finish.","","A 3D printer must follow a 3D CAD model, so saying it needs no design file is a misconception."]}
{"id":"gdt-168","s":"dt","t":"3.3.11","lv":1,"q":"A strip heater softens a line on a sheet of acrylic. The sheet is bent to 90° and held in a jig. Why is it held in the jig until it cools?","o":["So the solvent in the plastic can evaporate","So the plastic becomes more flexible again","So it cools and keeps its new shape","So the plastic shrinks back to a flat sheet"],"a":2,"x":"Acrylic is a thermoforming plastic. It hardens as it cools and keeps the bent shape. If released while hot, it can spring back. Cooling does not make it flexible.","w":["Acrylic does not set by solvent evaporation; this mixes it up with glues and paints.","Cooling makes thermoforming plastics stiffer, not more flexible. Heat is what makes them flexible.","","Holding it in the jig stops it going back to flat. Shrinking back is the very thing the jig prevents."]}
{"id":"gdt-169","s":"dt","t":"3.3.11","lv":1,"q":"A mild steel garden gate will stay outdoors all year. Which finish gives the best protection against rust?","o":["Powder coating","Clear beeswax polish","Tung oil","Sanding with fine abrasive paper"],"a":0,"x":"Powder coating bakes a tough plastic layer onto steel. It keeps out water and air, so the steel cannot rust. Waxes and oils suit timber, and sanding only smooths the surface.","w":["","Beeswax polish is a timber finish for indoor use. It would wear off steel outdoors.","Tung oil is a timber finish. It is not designed to protect steel from rust.","Sanding smooths the surface but is not a finish. Bare steel would still rust."]}
{"id":"gdt-170","s":"dt","t":"3.3.11","lv":2,"q":"A vacuum forming mould has sides that slope slightly inwards towards the top. What is the main reason for this?","o":["To make the plastic thicker at the sides","To trap air under the heated sheet","To make the sheet heat up more quickly","To let the mould be removed from the formed plastic"],"a":3,"x":"The slope is called a draft angle. The plastic shrinks onto the mould as it cools. Straight vertical sides would grip it and make removal hard. Air must be pulled out, not trapped.","w":["Slopes do not make the plastic thicker. Stretching the sheet over deep areas makes it thinner.","Air is sucked out through small holes so the plastic is pulled onto the mould. Trapped air would spoil the shape.","The heating happens before the sheet touches the mould, so the mould shape has no effect on heating time.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-171","s":"dt","t":"3.3.11","lv":2,"q":"A student drills a 6 mm hole in a mild steel bar on a pillar drill. Which action is both the safest and the most accurate?","o":["Hold the bar firmly by hand while wearing thick gloves","Centre punch the mark and clamp the bar in a machine vice","Leave the chuck key in the chuck so it is quick to find","Remove the drill guard so the drill point can be seen"],"a":1,"x":"A centre punch dent stops the drill wandering, and a clamped bar cannot spin. Gloves can be caught by rotating parts. The chuck key must be removed and the guard kept in place before drilling.","w":["An unclamped bar can spin and cause injury. Gloves can be caught by the drill, so they are not worn.","","A chuck key left in can be thrown out at high speed when the drill starts. Always remove it first.","Guards protect the user from swarf and moving parts. Removing one is unsafe and does not improve accuracy."]}
{"id":"gdt-172","s":"dt","t":"3.3.11","lv":3,"q":"An injection mould costs £12 000. Each plastic part costs £1.50 in material. Spreading the mould cost over 8000 parts gives £3.00 each. What is the cost per part if 16 000 are made?","o":["£3.00","£1.50","£2.25","£0.75"],"a":2,"x":"Mould cost per part is £12 000 ÷ 16 000 = £0.75. Add the £1.50 material to get £2.25. The trap is forgetting that the one-off mould cost is shared out over more parts.","w":["This is the cost for 8000 parts. Making more parts shares the mould cost further, so the cost per part falls.","This is the material cost only. It leaves out the share of the mould cost.","","This is only the mould cost share per part. It leaves out the £1.50 material cost."]}
{"id":"gdt-173","s":"dt","t":"mat-tb","lv":1,"q":"Why is natural timber seasoned before it is used to make furniture?","o":["To make the timber softer so it is easier to cut","To lower the moisture content so it warps and splits less","To make the timber waterproof for outdoor use","To glue the fibres together so the timber is stronger"],"a":1,"x":"Seasoning dries the timber, either in air or in a kiln. Wet timber shrinks as it dries and can warp, twist or split. Seasoning does not make timber waterproof or add glue.","w":["Seasoning does not soften timber; dry timber is usually harder to cut, so this is a mix-up about the purpose.","","Seasoned timber still absorbs water; only a finish or preservative gives protection, so this confuses seasoning with treating.","Natural timber has no added glue; this confuses seasoning with making a manufactured board."]}
{"id":"gdt-174","s":"dt","t":"mat-tb","lv":2,"q":"A pine shelf costs less than an oak shelf of the same size. Which reason best explains this?","o":["Oak is a manufactured board, so it costs more to process","Oak is a softwood, and softwoods are rare","Oak is much softer than pine, so it is worth more","Oak grows slowly, so trees take many years to mature"],"a":3,"x":"Oak is a hardwood from a slow-growing broad-leaved tree, so it takes longer to produce. Pine is a fast-growing conifer, so it is cheaper. The trap is mixing up hardwoods, softwoods and manufactured boards.","w":["Oak is a natural timber, not a manufactured board; this mixes up natural and manufactured timbers.","Oak is a hardwood, not a softwood, and softwoods like pine are plentiful and fast growing.","Oak is generally harder and denser than pine, not softer, so this reverses the property.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-175","s":"dt","t":"nea-A","lv":1,"q":"A student is designing a bedside lamp for an older relative. Which of these is primary research?","o":["Watching the relative use their current lamp and noting problems","Reading customer reviews of lamps on a shopping website","Reading a library book about lighting design","Reading a magazine article about lamp styles"],"a":0,"x":"Primary research is information you collect yourself, such as by observing or interviewing a user. Reviews, books and articles are secondary research because someone else collected the information.","w":["","Reviews were written by other people, so this is secondary research, not first-hand data.","A library book is a secondary source because the student did not collect the information.","A magazine article is secondary research because someone else gathered the information."]}
{"id":"gdt-176","s":"dt","t":"nea-A","lv":1,"q":"AQA releases contextual challenges for the NEA. What does a student do with them?","o":["Design the exact product that the challenge names","Choose one challenge and find a real user need within it","Wait for the teacher to set one challenge for the whole class","Copy an existing product that fits the challenge"],"a":1,"x":"The challenges are broad contexts. The student chooses one, then identifies a real user need or design opportunity inside it. The trap is thinking the challenge names a set product.","w":["The challenges are broad contexts, not set products, so students must find their own problem.","","Each student chooses their own challenge, so assuming the teacher decides is wrong.","The NEA needs an original design meeting a need, so copying a product shows no investigation."]}
{"id":"gdt-177","s":"dt","t":"nea-A","lv":2,"q":"A student chose a contextual challenge about living spaces. Which choice of client gives the best start for investigating needs?","o":["Everyone who has a kitchen at home","An imaginary person with no real details","A grandparent with weak grip who can be interviewed and observed","A kitchen tool shop with no contact with real users"],"a":2,"x":"A real client who can be interviewed, observed and asked for feedback gives strong evidence of needs. The trap is choosing a user group that is too broad, imaginary or not the end user.","w":["This group is far too broad, so the research has no clear focus.","An imagined user cannot give feedback, so any needs found are only guesses.","","A shop is not the end user, and without real users the student cannot find real needs."]}
{"id":"gdt-178","s":"dt","t":"nea-A","lv":2,"q":"A survey of 20 cyclists found: 15 said lights get stolen, 4 said batteries run flat, 1 wanted more colours. Which design opportunity do the results support most?","o":["A light with a longer-lasting battery","A light made in a wider choice of colours","A cheaper light made from thinner plastic","A light that is quick to remove or can be locked on"],"a":3,"x":"Most users (15 of 20) reported theft, so the strongest opportunity is a light that is easy to remove or secure. The trap is choosing a minority result or an idea with no evidence.","w":["Only 4 of 20 mentioned batteries, a smaller need than theft.","Only 1 person mentioned colour, so this is the weakest need in the results.","Nobody mentioned price or plastic thickness, so the results give no evidence for this.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-179","s":"dt","t":"nea-A","lv":3,"q":"A student is designing a kitchen tool for people over 75. They have only surveyed 30 classmates aged 14. What is the best way to improve the research?","o":["Interview and observe people over 75 using existing tools","Survey another 30 classmates so the sample is larger","Ask classmates which tool looks best and design that","Use only online reviews, as they are more reliable"],"a":0,"x":"The research must reflect the real users. Interviewing and observing people over 75 gives first-hand evidence of their needs. The trap: a bigger sample of the wrong group is still the wrong group.","w":["","More classmates still do not match the target users, so a bigger sample does not fix the mismatch.","Classmates' taste in looks does not show the needs of older users.","Reviews are secondary, may not be from this user group, and are not more reliable than first-hand research."]}
{"id":"gdt-180","s":"dt","t":"nea-B","lv":1,"q":"A student writes a design brief for their NEA project. Which statement describes what a good design brief should contain?","o":["A full list of sizes, materials and tolerances for the final product","A step-by-step plan of how and when each part will be made","A short statement of the need, who the user is and what you will design and make","A detailed drawing of the final design with a cutting list"],"a":2,"x":"A brief is a short statement of the problem or need, the user and what you will make. Detailed sizes, drawings and plans come later, in the specification, design and planning stages.","w":["Detailed sizes and tolerances belong in the specification, so this mixes up the brief and the specification.","A making plan is a later production schedule, not part of the design brief.","","A detailed drawing gives the solution too early, which is a design idea rather than a brief."]}
{"id":"gdt-181","s":"dt","t":"nea-B","lv":2,"q":"A student wants to help Year 11 students keep revision notes tidy on small desks. Which design brief leaves enough room to develop several different ideas?","o":["Design and make a pine bookcase with three shelves, 900 mm tall.","Design and make a product to keep revision notes tidy on a small desk.","Make a copy of the shelf unit that my sister bought for her room.","Design and make something useful for my bedroom."],"a":1,"x":"A good brief names the need and the user but does not fix the answer. The pine bookcase and the copy already decide the solution. 'Something useful' has no clear need or user.","w":["This already fixes the product, material and size, so naming the solution too early stops you exploring ideas.","","Copying an existing product gives no need or user and leaves no room for original ideas.","This is far too vague, naming no clear need or user to build a specification from."]}
{"id":"gdt-182","s":"dt","t":"nea-B","lv":2,"q":"Survey results show most users carry a product in a school bag all day. Which specification point is measurable and also gives a reason linked to this research?","o":["It should be light enough to carry around easily","It must be made from the lightest materials possible, as lighter is better","It must weigh under 500 g","It must weigh under 500 g, so it is comfortable to carry in a bag all day"],"a":3,"x":"A strong specification point can be tested and has a reason from research. 'Under 500 g' can be weighed, and the reason links to the survey. Without a reason, a point is weaker.","w":["'Light enough' cannot be measured and gives no reason from the research.","'Lightest possible' has no number to test against, and 'lighter is better' is not evidence from users.","This can be measured but gives no research-based reason, which the question asks for.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-183","s":"dt","t":"nea-B","lv":1,"q":"Why must a specification be written so that each point can be checked?","o":["It is used to test and judge the finished prototype against, in the evaluation","It gives the order and timings for making the prototype","It records the cost of the materials that were bought","It replaces the need to talk to users or carry out research"],"a":0,"x":"The specification is the list of criteria your prototype must meet. You test the prototype against it when you evaluate. If points cannot be checked, you cannot show whether the product succeeded.","w":["","Order and timings belong in a production plan or Gantt chart, not the specification.","Recording spending is the job of a costing sheet, not the purpose of a specification.","The specification should come from research with users rather than replace it."]}
{"id":"gdt-184","s":"dt","t":"nea-B","lv":3,"q":"A specification says a box must be 200 mm wide ± 2 mm. It must fit in a slot 204 mm wide. What is the smallest gap left if the box is made at the largest width allowed?","o":["6 mm","4 mm","2 mm","8 mm"],"a":2,"x":"The tolerance ±2 mm means the width can be 198 to 202 mm. The largest box is 202 mm. The smallest gap is 204 − 202 = 2 mm. The trap is using 200 mm or the smallest box.","w":["This uses the smallest box, 198 mm, which gives the largest gap, not the smallest.","This uses the nominal 200 mm width and ignores the tolerance.","","This uses a 196 mm width, but a ±2 mm tolerance only allows widths down to 198 mm."]}
{"id":"gdt-185","s":"dt","t":"nea-C","lv":1,"q":"A student uses CAD to show one of their design ideas. What is a good reason for using CAD at the ideas stage?","o":["The NEA must be drawn only with CAD","It replaces the need for a specification","It shows a 3D view and lets sizes or colours change quickly","It means the product needs no further drawings to be made"],"a":2,"x":"CAD can show an idea in 3D and allow quick changes to size, shape and colour. It is one of several ways to communicate ideas. Hand sketches and models are also allowed, so CAD is not compulsory.","w":["CAD is not compulsory, because hand sketching and modelling are also valid ways to show ideas.","The specification is still needed to judge ideas, and CAD does not replace it.","","Making still needs detailed drawings and plans, so an ideas-stage CAD image is not enough."]}
{"id":"gdt-186","s":"dt","t":"nea-C","lv":1,"q":"A student makes a quick card model of a desk tidy idea. What is the main benefit of this at the ideas stage?","o":["It shows if the size and shape work before an idea is chosen","It proves the final material will be strong enough","It means drawings and notes are no longer needed","It is the finished product, so no more work is needed"],"a":0,"x":"A quick model helps check size, shape and how an idea might feel to use. Card is not the final material, so it cannot prove strength. Models support drawings and notes rather than replacing them.","w":["","Card is not the final material, so a card model cannot reliably test strength.","Models support drawings and notes and do not replace the need to explain ideas.","A quick card model is only a test of an idea, not a finished product."]}
{"id":"gdt-187","s":"dt","t":"nea-C","lv":2,"q":"A student draws three desk lamp ideas. All have the same shape and the same switch. Only the colours differ. How could they best improve their range of ideas?","o":["Add more colours to each lamp","Make each lamp the same shape but larger","Draw the same three lamps more neatly","Vary the form, how it works and the materials"],"a":3,"x":"A range needs ideas that are genuinely different, not just recoloured. Changing the form, the working mechanism and the materials gives creative variety. Colour changes alone show very little thinking.","w":["More colours still leave the same idea, so the range is not creative.","A larger version is still the same idea, so it gives no real variety.","Neatness helps communication, but three similar ideas still lack range.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-188","s":"dt","t":"nea-C","lv":2,"q":"A student's first idea looks just like a product in the shops. They cannot think of anything different. What is the best way to get past this design fixation?","o":["Keep the first idea and add more detail","Look at unrelated sources, such as nature, and sketch quickly","Copy a competitor's product exactly","Stop at one idea and start making"],"a":1,"x":"Design fixation means sticking to the first idea. Using new sources of inspiration, such as nature, and sketching fast without judging helps to produce different ideas. Adding detail to one idea gives no range.","w":["Adding detail to the same idea keeps the fixation and gives no range.","","Copying is not creative and does not show original ideas.","One idea is not a range, and making should start only after ideas are developed."]}
{"id":"gdt-189","s":"dt","t":"nea-C","lv":3,"q":"Which idea sheet is most likely to earn marks in the top band for generating design ideas?","o":["Three similar ideas, one view each, with no notes","Six photos of existing products","Five varied ideas in sketches and CAD, annotated against the spec","One detailed CAD render with a long paragraph about it"],"a":2,"x":"Top marks need a range of creative, different ideas, communicated in different ways. Notes should explain how each idea meets the specification and could be made. Photos or a single idea do not show this.","w":["Similar ideas with no notes show little range, little creativity and no explanation.","Photos of existing products are not the student's own design ideas.","","One idea is not a range, so it cannot show creative variety however well described."]}
{"id":"gdt-190","s":"dt","t":"nea-D","lv":1,"q":"Why is it useful to make a quick card or foam model of a chosen idea before making the final prototype?","o":["It means testing can be skipped, because the model is already finished","It guarantees that the final prototype will earn full marks","It shows up problems with size, shape or fit cheaply, so they can be fixed early","It replaces the need to write a design brief and specification"],"a":2,"x":"A cheap model lets you find faults early and change the design before using the real materials. The trap is thinking a model is the finished product or a shortcut to marks.","w":["A model is made so that it can be tested, so skipping testing misses its whole point.","Nothing guarantees marks, which depend on how well you test, refine and justify your design.","","The brief and specification come first and guide the design, so a model cannot replace them."]}
{"id":"gdt-191","s":"dt","t":"nea-D","lv":1,"q":"Which piece of evidence best shows that a student has refined their design?","o":["A note saying the model was too tall, so height was cut from 300 mm to 240 mm","A neat, coloured presentation drawing of the student's first idea","A list of possible materials copied from a supplier's catalogue","A photograph of the finished prototype on the workshop bench"],"a":0,"x":"Refining means testing, finding a problem and making a justified change. A note linking test results to a specific change shows this. The trap is thinking neat drawings or a finished product show refinement.","w":["","A drawing of the first idea shows no testing and no change, so it is not refinement.","A catalogue list shows research, not testing a model and improving the design.","The finished prototype comes later and does not show how the design was tested and changed."]}
{"id":"gdt-192","s":"dt","t":"nea-D","lv":2,"q":"A card model of a tool handle is tested. Three adult users say it is too thin to grip comfortably. What is the best next step?","o":["Ignore the feedback, because card does not feel like the real material","Make the prototype anyway and mention the thin handle in the evaluation","Change the specification so that grip comfort is no longer required","Record the feedback, widen the handle using hand-size data, then test a new model"],"a":3,"x":"Good development records the test result, makes a justified change using anthropometric data, then retests. The trap is leaving a known fault to fix later or changing the spec to fit the fault.","w":["Size and grip can still be judged on a card model, so ignoring the feedback wastes the test.","A known fault should be fixed during development, not left until after the prototype is made.","The specification guides the design, so dropping a requirement to hide a fault is a mistake.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-193","s":"dt","t":"nea-D","lv":2,"q":"A student designs a night-light with a light sensor circuit. What is the best way to model and test the circuit before making a PCB?","o":["Solder the parts straight onto copper-clad board","Build it on a breadboard or in circuit simulation software and test it","Draw the circuit symbols and assume it will work","Cut out the case first and fit the parts afterwards"],"a":1,"x":"A breadboard or simulation lets you test the circuit and change component values without soldering. Drawing alone is not testing, and soldering first makes mistakes costly to fix.","w":["Soldering before testing makes faults hard and costly to correct because the joins are permanent.","","A drawing is not a test, so assuming it works lets faults go unnoticed.","Making the case does not test whether the circuit works, so circuit testing should come first."]}
{"id":"gdt-194","s":"dt","t":"nea-D","lv":3,"q":"A student tests two bag models. Version B has a wider strap and a different fabric. Users prefer B. Why is this a weak conclusion about the strap?","o":["Two versions is too few, as at least ten versions must be tested","Opinions from users cannot count as test evidence","Two things changed at once, so it is unclear which change users preferred","Fabric should only be changed after the final prototype is made"],"a":2,"x":"For a fair test, change one thing at a time. Here the strap and fabric both changed, so the preference could be due to either. The trap is blaming the number of versions or user opinions instead.","w":["There is no rule of ten versions; the problem is that more than one feature differs.","User opinions are valid evidence for comfort and appearance; the fault is the unfair test.","","Fabric can be tested on models during development, and waiting until the prototype is too late."]}
{"id":"gdt-195","s":"dt","t":"nea-E","lv":1,"q":"You are cutting a piece of pine to length with a tenon saw. Where should the saw blade go in relation to your marked line?","o":["On the centre of the line, so the cut is exactly in the middle","On the part side of the line, so you can sand it bigger later","On the waste side of the line, leaving a little to plane or sand to size","At least 10 mm away from the line, to keep your fingers safe"],"a":2,"x":"Cut on the waste side so a small amount is left to finish exactly to the line. Material cannot be added back. The saw also removes some wood (the kerf), so cutting on the line makes the part too short.","w":["Cutting down the centre removes part of the line and the kerf, so the part ends up slightly too short.","Cutting on the part side makes the piece undersize, and sanding removes wood rather than adding it back.","","Cutting 10 mm away wastes material and leaves far too much to remove. Safety comes from clamping, not a big gap."]}
{"id":"gdt-196","s":"dt","t":"nea-E","lv":1,"q":"You need eight identical acrylic pieces with the same curved edge for your prototype. Why is it sensible to make a template first?","o":["It lets you mark every piece the same, so they are accurate and consistent","It means you do not need to finish the edges of the pieces","It cuts the acrylic for you, so no tools are needed","It lets you ignore the tolerances on your drawing"],"a":0,"x":"A template or jig is used for repeatability. Each piece is marked or shaped to the same outline. You still need to finish edges, use tools and check sizes against your tolerances.","w":["","A template only guides the shape. Edges still need filing and polishing.","A template is only a guide. You still need tools to cut and shape the acrylic.","Tolerances still apply. A template helps you meet them but you must still check each piece."]}
{"id":"gdt-197","s":"dt","t":"nea-E","lv":2,"q":"A drawing says a spacer must be 80 mm ± 0.5 mm. You cut four spacers: 79.4 mm, 79.6 mm, 80.4 mm and 80.6 mm. Which spacers are outside tolerance?","o":["79.6 mm and 80.4 mm","80.4 mm and 80.6 mm","79.4 mm only","79.4 mm and 80.6 mm"],"a":3,"x":"The allowed range is 79.5 mm to 80.5 mm. 79.4 mm is too short and 80.6 mm is too long. The trap is forgetting that tolerance applies to both sides of the target size.","w":["These two are inside the range of 79.5 to 80.5 mm. This mixes up which values are acceptable.","80.4 mm is inside the range, and 79.4 mm is missed. This only looks at the upper end.","79.4 mm is too short, but 80.6 mm is too long as well. This only checks one end of the range.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-198","s":"dt","t":"nea-E","lv":2,"q":"Which evidence in your making record best shows you worked to accurate tolerances?","o":["One clear photo of the finished prototype from the front","Photos of you measuring parts, with sizes checked against the drawing","A list of all the tools you used in the workshop","A copy of the safety poster from the workshop wall"],"a":1,"x":"Evidence of checking during making, with measurements compared to the drawing and corrections made, shows accuracy. A final photo or a tool list does not show how accurately you worked. Record the checks as you go.","w":["A final photo shows the result but not the checks or measurements made during making.","","A list of tools says what you used, not how accurately you used it.","A safety poster shows nothing about your own accuracy or measuring."]}
{"id":"gdt-199","s":"dt","t":"nea-E","lv":3,"q":"Three blocks, each 40 mm ± 0.5 mm, go end to end in a slot that is 120 mm to 121 mm long. What is the greatest total length of the blocks, and will they always fit?","o":["120.5 mm, so they always fit","121.5 mm, so they always fit","121.5 mm, so they may not fit","123.0 mm, so they may not fit"],"a":2,"x":"Each block can be up to 40.5 mm, so three give 121.5 mm. The slot is at most 121 mm, so some sets will not fit. The trap is applying the ± 0.5 mm only once instead of to every block.","w":["This applies the tolerance only once, not to all three blocks, so the greatest length is wrongly low.","The total is right, but 121.5 mm is longer than the 121 mm maximum slot, so they will not always fit.","","This adds 1 mm to each block, but the tolerance is only 0.5 mm on each block, so the total is too big."]}
{"id":"gdt-200","s":"dt","t":"nea-F","lv":1,"q":"Your specification says a tablet stand must hold the tablet at 60° ±5°. Which test best checks this point?","o":["Rest a tablet on the stand and measure the angle with a protractor","Ask a friend if the stand looks about right","Compare the stand's price with shop-bought stands","Check that your drawings show 60°"],"a":0,"x":"A measurable spec point needs a measured test on the real prototype. Drawings show intent, not the result. Opinions and price do not check the angle.","w":["","An opinion on looks is not a measurement, and the spec gives a number you must measure.","Comparing prices tests cost, not the angle this spec point asks about.","Drawings show the plan, not the finished prototype, so the made product must be tested."]}
{"id":"gdt-201","s":"dt","t":"nea-F","lv":1,"q":"Why should you ask your intended users to try your prototype as part of your evaluation?","o":["Their opinions replace the need to test against the specification","Users always say a prototype is good, which proves it works","They can show whether it meets real needs in real use","It means you do not have to suggest any improvements"],"a":2,"x":"User feedback shows how the prototype works in real use for the people it is made for. It adds to testing against the specification. It does not replace it.","w":["User feedback adds to testing against the specification but can never replace it.","Users often point out faults, and praise alone does not prove a prototype works.","","Feedback usually reveals problems, giving you more improvements to suggest, not fewer."]}
{"id":"gdt-202","s":"dt","t":"nea-F","lv":2,"q":"The spec says a shelf must support 20 N. In testing it held 15 N, then the joint cracked at 18 N. What is the best conclusion?","o":["Met, because it held 15 N and 18 N is close enough","Met, as long as you change the specification to 15 N","Not met, but it still works so no change is needed","Not met, as it failed below 20 N; strengthen joint and retest"],"a":3,"x":"The shelf failed before reaching 20 N, so the spec point is not met. A good evaluation says so, suggests a fix such as a stronger joint, then retests. Changing the spec hides the failure.","w":["Close is not a pass, because the spec says 20 N and the joint failed at 18 N.","Rewriting the spec to suit the result hides the failure instead of judging against the original spec.","A cracked joint is a failure, so saying no change is needed ignores the test result.",""]}
{"id":"gdt-203","s":"dt","t":"nea-F","lv":2,"q":"A test showed a wooden drawer stuck when it was opened in damp conditions. Which is the best improvement to write in your evaluation?","o":["Make the drawer better so that it works well in all conditions","Plane 1 mm off the runners and apply wax, then retest in damp conditions","Use a more expensive wood and hope the problem goes away","Start the whole project again from the first design idea"],"a":1,"x":"A strong improvement is specific, linked to the test result, practical and testable. Planing and waxing the runners targets the sticking. Vague or extreme suggestions do not.","w":["This is too vague because it names no actual change and is not linked to the test result.","","This is a costly guess that does not target the cause of the sticking.","Starting again is extreme and unnecessary when a small modification can fix the fault."]}
{"id":"gdt-204","s":"dt","t":"nea-F","lv":3,"q":"Your spec says a handle must be comfortable for left- and right-handed users. Five users test it. Four find it comfortable. The one left-handed user finds it awkward. What should you do?","o":["Record the point as not fully met and redesign the grip to be symmetrical","Record it as met, because most users were happy","Ignore the result, as one person is not enough evidence","Remove left-handed users from the specification"],"a":0,"x":"The spec needs the handle to suit both groups, so one left-handed failure shows it is not fully met. Majority opinion does not meet the spec. Suggest a symmetrical grip and retest.","w":["","A majority is not enough because the spec says both left- and right-handed users must be comfortable.","Dismissing the only left-handed user's result ignores evidence of a real weakness against the spec.","Changing the spec to avoid a failure hides the problem instead of improving the prototype."]}
